"Passage 1:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:52 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nBut in fact, in this sordid affair, it was my son Ellery who showed his cleverness. He's a smart kid.\" [2137] The officer sniffed his snuff and leaned comfortably in his armchair, Sampson and Cronin relaxed, and Juno curled up in the corner, listening intently. [2138] \"Looking back on the Field case,\" the officer began, \"I sometimes have to mention Benjamin Morgan, who was truly the most innocent victim of all. I want you to remember, Henry, whatever I say about Morgan, that's it. Don't say anything, either at work or in social circles. I've already made Tim promise to keep it secret... \"\n[2139] The two men nodded silently. The officer continued, \"Most criminal investigations begin by looking for a motive, which goes without saying. Many times, once you know the reason behind the crime, you can eliminate the suspects one by one. In this case, the motive has been unclear for a long time. Although there were some signs, such as Benjamin Morgan's secrets, they were not conclusive. Morgan had been blackmailed by Field for a long time - gentlemen, although you know Field's other social habits, you may not know some of his activities. This seems to suggest that blackmail was a possible motive - or to get rid of blackmail. However, many things could also become motives - for example, revenge by a criminal who was 'sent to prison' by Field; or by a member of his criminal gang. Field had many enemies, of course, and also many friends, but only because he held their leashes. Many people - both men and women - had a motive to kill this lawyer. That night at the Roman Theater, we had too many urgent things to consider and solve, so we didn't bother with the motive. It was always lurking behind the scenes, waiting to appear. [2140] \"But please note this: if this were blackmail - as Ellery and I eventually concluded, because this possibility seemed the greatest - then at least this can be said: Field must have had some revealing documents in his possession. We know that Morgan's documents existed. Cronin insisted that the documents he was looking for were somewhere. So we had to stay alert and look for documents - tangible evidence that might shed light on the underlying situation of the crime.\" [2141] \"Meanwhile, in terms of documents, Ellery noticed that there were many books on handwriting analysis in Field's belongings. We can conclude that, as we know, Field had blackmailed (for example, in the Morgan case) and had a suspicion of multiple cases; coupled with his strong interest in handwriting analysis, a person like him would likely forge handwriting. If so, and if it can be explained, it may mean that Field had the habit of forging the original blackmail documents. He did so, of course, to sell the forgeries and retain the originals for further blackmail. His connections with the underworld undoubtedly made him familiar with the tricks of the trade. Later, we found that this assumption was correct. At that time, we determined that blackmail was the motive for this crime. But remember, this led us astray, because any suspect could have been the victim of blackmail, and we did not know who it was. [2142] The officer frowned and adjusted his posture. [2143] \"But I handled this explanation wrongly, which shows how habits can control a person. I always start with the motive\u2026 but in the investigation, the only important, core thing that was visible was a jumbled mess of clues \u2013 or rather, the lack of clues. I mean the lost hat\u2026\n[2144] \"Unfortunately, on Monday night at the Roman Theater, we were in such a hurry to question immediately that we overlooked the significance of the lost hat. It's not that we weren't puzzled by it at first \u2013 far from it, it was one of the first things I noticed when I examined the body. As for Ellery, he noticed it when he bent down to check the body. But