 Field, [2183] the person who left the theater on Monday night wearing a full suit and a silk hat of Field, [2184] the person who left the theater on Monday night wearing a full suit and a silk hat of Field, [2185] the person who left the theater on Monday night wearing a full suit and a silk hat of Field, [2186] the person who left the theater on Monday night wearing a full suit and a silk hat of Field, [2187] the person who left the theater on Monday night wearing a full suit and a silk hat of Field, [2188] the person who left the theater on Monday night wearing a full suit and a silk hat of Field, [2189] the person who left the theater on Monday night wearing a full suit and a silk hat of Field, [2190] the person who left the theater on Monday night wearing a full suit and a silk hat of Field, [2191] the person who left the theater on Monday night wearing a full suit and a silk hat of Field, [2192] the person who left the theater on Monday night wearing a full suit and a silk hat of Field, [2193] the person who left the theater on Monday night wearing a full suit and a silk hat of Field, [2194] the person who left the theater on Monday night wearing a full suit and a silk hat of Field, [2195] the person who left the theater on Monday night wearing a full suit and a silk hat of Field, [2196] the person who left the theater on Monday night wearing a full suit and a silk hat of Field, [2197] the person who left the theater on Monday night wearing a full suit and a silk hat of Field, [2198]\n[2157] \"If this person is the killer \u2013 it couldn't be anyone else \u2013 then our search area is greatly reduced. He could only be one of the male actors wearing evening attire who left, or someone closely associated with the theater and dressed similarly. This person would first need to have a prop hat that must be left behind; secondly, have access to the costume storage room and dressing room without being detected; and finally, have the opportunity to leave his prop hat in the costume storage room and dressing room. [2158] \"Now let's analyze the possibility of the second scenario - the killer is closely related to the theater but not an actor.\" The policeman paused, taking a deep breath of his cherished snuff, \"The backstage staff can be ruled out as none of them wore evening gowns, which are necessary to take away Field's high hat. Similarly, the cashier, usher, doorman, and other minor staff can also be ruled out. The promoter Harry Nelson was dressed in ordinary civilian clothes. Manager Poulson wore a full suit, yes, but I measured his hat size and found it to be six and three-quarters - an unusually small size. He couldn't wear Field's hat, which was seven and three-quarters. We left the theater before him, that's right. But on my way out, I specifically instructed Thomas Wiley to search Poulson as thoroughly as he would search others. Earlier that evening, I checked Poulson's hat in his office out of a sense of responsibility and found it to be a round top hat. Wiley later reported that Poulson left with this top hat, without taking any other hats. Now - if Poulson were the person we were looking for, he might have left with Field's hat, although it was too big for him to wear, but he could have carried it. However, he left with a top hat, so there is no doubt that he could not have taken Field's hat, as the door was locked when he left the theater, and no one - I can guarantee with my staff on duty - entered the theater until Thursday morning when I opened the door myself. Theoretically, anyone who could hide Field's hat in the theater, such as Poulson or any other employee, could be the killer. But this last assumption was refuted by the report of our official building expert, Edmund Crow. He clearly stated that there are no secret hiding places in the Roman Theater. [2159] \"Having eliminated Parelli, Nelsen, and the staff, there was only the possibility of the actors left. I'll leave it to you to guess how we narrowed down the field to Barry. The most intriguing part of this case, in fact, was a series of surprising and intricate deductions that led to the truth. I should say 'we' \u2014 but really, I should say 'Eliot'\u2026\"\n[2160] \"As a detective, you are quite the bashful one,\" Crooning chuckled, \"Oh, this is more thrilling than a detective story. I should be working now, but my boss seems equally interested \u2013 go on, officer!\" [2161] Quinn smiled and continued speaking. [2162] \"The fact that the killer's identity was traced to an actor,\" he continued, \"answered a question that may have occurred to you and has been bothering us from the start. At first, we didn't understand why the theater was chosen as the meeting place for the secret deal. You stop to think, and in general, the theater has many inconveniences. Just one thing: to ensure privacy, you have to buy several extra tickets around the seats to make them empty. Compared to other more convenient meeting places, choosing such a chaotic place is simply ridiculous! The theater is mostly dark and quiet, which is unsettling. Any unexpected sound or conversation would be noticed. The audience is also a potential danger \u2013 they could be recognized. However, when you realize that Barry is an actor, all these problems are solved. From his perspective, the theater is ideal \u2013 because when the victim is found dead in the front row of the stalls, who would suspect the actor as the killer? Of course, Field consented to this arrangement, not realizing what Barry was thinking, and not realizing that he was conspiring in his own death. You must remember, he dealt with dangerous people all the time, even if he had some suspicions, he thought he could handle it. This might have made him a little too confident \u2013 of course, we'll never know. [2163] \"Let's go back to Ellery - my favorite topic,\" the detective continued, chuckling as usual, \"Besides these deductions about the hat - actually, before these deductions appeared - Ellery had noticed suspicious signs at the Evans-Pope house during their meeting. It was clear that during the intermission, Field had approached Frances Evans-Pope in the alley not just for flirting. In Ellery's view, there was some kind of connection between these two distant individuals. However, this did not mean that Frances had to know about this connection. She was absolutely certain that she had never heard of or seen Field before. We have no reason to suspect her, but we have ample reason to believe her.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 2:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:52 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nIf Stephen Barry and Field knew each other, and Frances was unaware, the possible link might be Stephen Barry. For example, if Field had an appointment with the actor on Monday evening and suddenly saw Frances, he might have dared to approach her, especially if the topic between him and Barry was related to her. As for why she recognized her - thousands of people who read the daily newspaper are familiar with her face - she is a young socialite who is often photographed. Field, with his comprehensive business methods, naturally knew her appearance and appearance... But let's go back to this triangle relationship - Field, Frances, Barry - I will explain in detail later. Through photos and other news channels, everyone knows that Barry is engaged to Frances and is her fianc\u00e9. You must have realized that, in this case, among the actors, no one else can answer this question: why did Field approach Frances? [2164] \"There is another puzzling factor about Frances - her purse was found in Field's clothes - a reasonable explanation is that, when the inebriated lawyer approached her, she dropped it out of nervousness. Later, Jesse Lynch's testimony also corroborated this, as his testimony suggested that he saw Field pick up Frances' purse. Poor girl - I feel sorry for her.\" The officer sighed. [2165] \"Let's go back to the hat\u2014you'll notice we've always been mentioning that damn hat.\" Quin paused, then continued, \"I've never seen a case where a single factor has played a part in every aspect of an investigation\u2026Now, pay attention to this: out of all the cast members, Barry was the only one who left the Roman Theater on Monday night wearing a tuxedo and a top hat. On Monday night, Ellery noticed as people were leaving the theater that everyone else in the cast was dressed in casual attire; in fact, later, when he was in Pencroft's office, he mentioned this to Sampson and me, but we didn't fully realize its significance at the time\u2026Therefore, Barry was the only cast member who could have taken Field's hat. By using Ellery's reasoning about the hat, we can now cut through the fog and conclude that Barry is the suspect. [2166] \"The next step we need to do is to see the performance ourselves. That evening - Thursday, Ellery made a decisive inference. You'll understand why. We wanted to see if Barry had time to commit the crime during the second act to confirm our conclusion. It was surprising that out of all the cast members, only Barry had the time. He left the stage at 9:20 - he went on stage, and then left almost immediately - and didn't return until 9:50, staying on stage until the act ended. This was an undeniable part of the fixed schedule. The other actors were either on stage throughout or had very short intervals between going on and off stage. This means that on Thursday night, which was five days ago, we solved the mystery - the whole case took nine days to solve, but figuring out the identity of the killer and bringing him to justice were two different things. You'll understand in a moment.\" [2167] \"The killer wouldn't be able to enter until around 9:30, which explains why the edges torn off of LL32 left and LL30 left are not consistent. You know, Field and Barry had to come in at different times. Field wouldn't be able to come in with Barry, or even be too obviously late \u2013 it was too important for Barry to keep the secret, and Field understood, or he thought he understood the necessity of adhering to the rules of secrecy.\" [2168] \"When we determined Barry to be a suspect on Thursday evening, we decided to subtly interrogate the other members of the cast and the staff behind the scenes. Of course, we wanted to find out if anyone had truly seen Barry leave or return. Coincidentally, no one had. Everyone was either busy with the performance, changing costumes, or working backstage. This investigation was conducted after the show ended that night and Barry had left the theater. This approach turned out to be quite effective. [2169] \"We borrowed a seating chart from Poulson. With this chart, along with a quick check of the left aisle after the second act on Thursday night, and the layout of the dressing rooms on stage \u2013 we can figure out how the killer did it.\" [2170] Sampson was visibly excited. \"I've been wrestling with this for quite some time,\" he admitted, \"after all, Field isn't a fool. This Barry must be a genius, Kwai, how did he do it?\" [2171] \"Once the mystery is unraveled, any riddle is simple,\" the detective answered. \"After 9:20, Barry had free time, so he quickly went back to the dressing room, did a thorough disguise on his face, put on a cloak, and a high hat, which was part of his costume - you all remember, he was wearing evening dress - and then slipped out of the dressing room and into the alley. Of course, you don't know the layout of the theater. There are many floor levels facing the left alley on the backstage side of the theater, which are dressing rooms. Barry's dressing room was on the ground floor, with a door facing the alley. There is an iron staircase leading down to the sidewalk. [2172] \"He left the dressing room through this door, slipped through the dark alley when the side door was closed during the second act. He sneaked out onto the street when no one was guarding the end of the alley - luckily for him, Jesse Lynch and his 'girl' hadn't arrived yet. Then he walked boldly into the theater as if he were late. He showed his ticket at the door - LL30 left, wrapped in a cloak, of course disguised very well. He entered the theater and deliberately threw away the stub. This was a wise move on his part, as he thought that if they found the stub there, it would only arouse suspicion in a certain audience member, not on the stage. Moreover, if his plan failed, he would be searched thoroughly later, and the stub found on him would be irrefutable evidence. Ultimately, he thought his actions would not only divert attention but also protect him. [2173] \"But how did he find his seat without a usher? If there had been an usher, he would have been seen. \"The detective answered, \"He didn't intend to avoid the usher. Since the performance was going on, the theater was dark, and he naturally hoped that the usher hadn't arrived yet and sat in the last row closest to the door. Even if the usher arrived before him and led him to his seat, with his disguise and the darkness of the theater, he wouldn't be recognized. Therefore, in the worst case, others would only remember that a man came in during the second act of the performance, and even his general outline would be difficult to describe. Coincidentally, no one approached him because Maggie O'Connell was luckily sitting next to her sweetheart. So he could sit next to Field without being noticed.\" [2174] \"But how did he find his seat without an usher? If there had been an usher, he would have been seen.\" Croaning disbelievingly. [2175] \"He had no intention of avoiding the usher,\" the officer replied, \"since it was during the performance and the theater was dimly lit, he naturally hoped the usher had not yet arrived and was seated in the back row closest to the entrance. However, even if the usher arrived before him and led him to his seat, due to his good disguise and the dark theater, he would not be recognized. Therefore, in the worst-case scenario, others' recollections of him would be of a man who entered during the second act, with a body outline that was barely describable. Coincidentally, no one approached him because Marcie O'Connell was fortunately sitting next to her sweetheart. Thus, he was able to sit quietly next to Field without being noticed. [2176] \"Please remember, what I just said is not a deduction or investigation result. We don't have the ability to discover these facts. Barry's confession last night revealed these details... Once we knew the criminal was Barry, we could deduce the whole process... If you know the criminal, it's simple and normal. However, this is not necessary for solving the case. Does this sound like an excuse for Ellery or me? Hmmph!\" The old man forced a smile. [2177] \"He had a well-planned plan when he sat next to Field,\" the officer cleared his throat, continuing. \"He had to strictly follow the schedule and not waste a minute. Moreover, Field knew that Barry had to return, so he didn't delay. Barry told us that he thought it would be difficult to deal with Field, but it wasn't as difficult as he thought. Field was amiable and accepted Barry's suggestion, perhaps because he had drunk too much and wanted to get that large sum of money quickly. [2178] \"Barry first asked for the documents. Field cunningly demanded money before showing the documents, and Barry showed him a wallet that was obviously full of real bills. The theater was very dark, and Barry didn't take out the bills. In fact, these were the bills used in the performance on stage. Barry hinted at the bills, but he wanted to check the documents first before paying. Field was probably expecting this. We know that Barry is a successful actor, and his training on stage allows him to handle difficult situations with ease...\n[2179] \"Imagine his reaction when Field reached under his seat and pulled out his high hat. Barry confessed that at the time, Field said, 'Didn't you expect me to hide the documents here? In fact, I specifically use this hat to store your records. Look - here is your name.' He said this while flipping the hatband over! Barry saw his name written in ink on the inside of the hatband with his flashlight. [2180] \"Imagine his brain's reaction at that moment. His meticulous plan was about to be ruined by this hat. If his hat had been checked when his body was found - of course, it would have been checked - the name 'Stephen Barry' on the hatband would have been the ironclad evidence... Barry didn't have time to remove the hatband. First, he didn't have a knife - unfortunately\n[2181] \"Sensitive mind, Henry, sensitive mind,\" Quinn said gravely, \"many have gone to the gallows... just as he was about to take the hat, he realized he couldn't leave his own there. For one, his hat was a fedora \u2013 a foldable, wide-brimmed hat \u2013 but more importantly, it bore the name of the costume supplier, L\u00e9on Broun. You can see, this would immediately point to someone in the production \u2013 which was exactly what he wanted to avoid. He told me, at the time and for some time after, he thought the police could only infer that something valuable was hidden in the hat. He couldn't fathom how this line of investigation would implicate him. When I explained to him that Ellery simply made deductions based on the fact of the lost hat, he was completely astounded... Now you understand, his only fundamental flaw in the commission of the crime was not negligence or error, but the unforeseen occurrence that forced him to act, which in turn set off a chain reaction. If Barry's name hadn't been on Field's hat, I wouldn't have had any suspicions, and until today, he would have been a free man with no suspicion. The police files would have added another unsolved murder case. [2182] \"As you can imagine, these thoughts flashed through his mind in a moment, not as long as I've described. He knew what he had to do, and his plans had to be adjusted immediately due to the new developments... After Field retrieved the documents from the hat, Barry quickly checked them under the watchful gaze of the lawyer. He used the same mini flashlight, which was dimmed due to the obstruction from the two of them. The documents appeared to be well-organized and complete. However, Barry didn't spend much time on them at that moment. He lifted his head and said, \"Seems like everything's here, damn it\u2014\" in a natural tone, as if they were already reconciled enemies, and he was a magnanimous person. Field didn't understand the true meaning of this statement. Barry reached into his pocket \u2013 the flashlight was off at that time \u2013 and appeared to be nervous, taking a gulp of the premium whisky from the bottle. He then seemed to realize he was being impolite and asked Field if he would like to take a sip to celebrate the successful transaction. Seeing Barry drink from the bottle, Field no longer had any suspicions. In fact, he probably never imagined that Barry would try to eliminate him. Barry handed him the bottle...\n[2183] \"But it wasn't the same bottle. Under the cover of darkness, he took out two bottles - the one he drank from was taken out of his left back pocket, while the one he gave to Field was taken out of his right back pocket. It was simple - it was dark there, and the lawyer was already drunk, so it was even simpler. The bottle trick worked. But Barry was thorough. He had a syringe filled with poison in his pocket. If Field refused to drink, Barry was prepared to inject the needle into Field's arm or thigh. He had an injection needle given to him by a doctor years ago. Barry had suffered from neurasthenia but could not undergo normal treatment by the doctor because he had to perform with the troupe in various places. Since the syringe was old, its origin could not be traced; Barry was also prepared if Field did not drink. You see - even in such details, his plan was flawless...\"\n[2184] \"The bottle of Scotch that Field drank contained high-quality whiskey, that's true, but it was heavily laced with tetraethyl lead. The strong smell of the liquor masked the subtle ether taste of the poison; Field had taken a large gulp before realizing something was amiss - if he had realized at all. [2185] \"He handed the bottle back to Barry without a second thought, and Barry pocketed it, saying, 'I think I need to look over these documents more closely - I don't trust you, Field...' Field was by now so dull-witted that he simply nodded his head blankly, then collapsed onto his seat. Barry truly examined the documents, but all the while kept an eagle's eye on Field with the corner of his eye. After about five minutes, he saw that Field was finished - completely finished. He wasn't completely unconscious, but he was close; his face contorted, and he couldn't breathe. He seemed unable to move or cry out. Of course, in his agony, he forgot all about Barry, and soon he was completely unconscious. When he spoke his last words to Basker, it was merely the dying flicker of a flame...\n[2186] \"Barry looked at his watch. It was 9:40, he had only been with Field for ten minutes. He had to be back on stage at 9:50. He decided to wait another three minutes - this took less time than he had anticipated - to ensure that Field wouldn't make any more noise. At 9:43, Field was in agony and on the brink of death, Barry picked up Field's hat, quickly removed his own hat and tucked it under his cloak, stood up. He was familiar with the route. He stuck to the wall, carefully and inconspicuously making his way along the aisle to the back of the left box. The play was at its climax, and everyone was focused on the stage, none of them noticing him. [2187] \"Behind the box, he tore off the wig, quickly adjusted his makeup, and walked through the stage door. This door led to a narrow passage, which led to a corridor, which then turned into different areas of the stage area. His dressing room was only a few feet away from the entrance of the corridor. He slipped in, tossing his prop hat into his ordinary items, pouring the remaining poison from the bottle into the sink, and washing the bottle. He poured the remaining liquid from the syringe into the toilet, and put away the cleaned needle. If he was caught - what did it matter? He had a valid reason for having the syringe, and besides, he hadn't used it in the murder case... He was now ready to go on stage, calm, happy, and confident, but also slightly annoyed. At exactly 9:50, he was called to the stage. He walked up to the stage, and at 9:55, there was a shout from the front row of the auditorium... \"\n[2188] \"Discuss your complex plans,\" said Sampson suddenly. [2189] \"It's not as complicated as it first sounded,\" the officer replied, \"Remember, Barry is a very intelligent and accomplished young man, especially an excellent actor. Only an experienced actor could pull off a plan like this. After all, the process is simple; the hardest part is sticking to the schedule. If he was seen, he would have been disguised. The most dangerous part of his plan was \u2013 how to escape when he walked down the aisle, through the backstage entrance to the stage, while the ushers were there. He had observed the ushers while sitting next to Field. Of course, he knew that due to the nature of the performance, the ushers would be mostly faithful to their duties, and he counted on his disguise and syringe to help him through any unexpected situations. However, Maggie O'Connell did not do her job properly, so that was to his advantage. He proudly told me last night that he had prepared for emergencies... As for the backstage entrance, he knew from experience that everyone would be on stage and the technicians would be busy in their positions at that time... Remember, he planned the crime with the exact conditions in mind. If there were any dangers and uncertainties \u2013 well, isn't that the nature of an adventure? \u2013 he asked me with a smile last night; I had to admit that, if not for this, I would greatly admire his style.\" [2190] The officer uneasily shifted the topic. \"I hope I've made it clear how Barry committed the crime. As for our investigation... besides the reasoning about the hat and knowing the identity of the killer, we still know nothing about the exact details of the crime. If you remember the evidence we gathered on Thursday night, you will understand that there was nothing to go on. What we hope most is that there is some clue in the files we need to find that is related to Barry. Even then, it's not enough... so the next step, the officer sighed, \"is to find the secret compartment on the canopy of the bed in Field's apartment. This work has been done by Ellery from beginning to end. We found that Field did not have a safe, no mailbox, no other residence, no intimate neighbors or business partners, and those files were not in his office. After elimination, Ellery insisted that these files were in Field's room. You know how the search ended - completely through Ellery's genius reasoning. We found Morgan's files, discovered the material needed by Croning about the mafia activities - by the way, Tim, I keenly noticed what happened when we started the big clean-up - we finally found a bundle of miscellaneous files, including Michaels and Barry's... Tim, you will remember, Ellery deduced from handwriting analysis that we might find Barry's original files - and indeed we did. [2191] \"Mickles' case was quite interesting.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 3:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:52 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \n\"How did the murderer commit the crime?\" Mr. Queen agreed with my point of view, and the quick-witted detective novel researcher should now be able to draw a clear conclusion to these questions at this stage of the story. The answer \u2013 or evidence that accurately points to the criminal \u2013 can be derived through a series of logical reasoning and psychological observations... Let me conclude with a warning to the reader: \"Reader, beware!\" [1968] J.J.McC. [1969] This book is presented to you by ePUBw.COM. com\n[1970] Part Four\n[1971] \"The perfect criminal is like a superhero. He must be meticulous in his methods: he is a lone wolf who cannot be detected and is invisible. He has no friends and is alone. He must be careful not to make mistakes, must have a sharp mind and quick reflexes ... but that doesn't matter, such a person still exists ... On the other hand, he must be favored by fate - even if he faces uncontrollable situations, he will not be ruined. I think this is even more difficult ... But the last point is the hardest. He must never repeat his crime, the weapon he used, or the motive for his crime! ... In my forty years of police career in the United States, I have not encountered or investigated such a perfect criminal. [1972] - Excerpt from Richard Queen: \"Crime and Investigation Methods in America\"\n[1973] Chapter 19: Queen's Further Interrogation\n[1974] On Saturday night, Richard Queen's behavior was unexpected and surprising, especially for the district attorney, Sampson. The old man became irritable and angry, completely unreasonable. He bit his lips, muttered to himself, and paced nervously around Louis Pincer's office. He didn't even notice the presence of Sampson, Pincer, and another person. That person had never appeared in the theater office before. He sat in Pincer's large chair like a mouse, with eyes as big as teacups. This bright-eyed person was Zuna, who accompanied his melancholy master to the Roman Theater to investigate the case, a rare honor. [1975] In fact, Queen's mood was very low. Throughout his career, he had encountered countless clearly unsolvable problems and turned them around. Therefore, the strange behavior of the police officer was even more puzzling to Sampson. He had dealt with the old man for many years and had never seen him so agitated. [1976] The old man's mood was not due to the progress of the investigation of the Field case, as Sampson feared. Juana sat with his mouth open in the corner, the only one who knew why the officer was pacing nervously. With his quick wits and innate insight, as well as his long-standing friendly relationship with the old man, Juana was very familiar with his temperament. He knew that his master's actions were only because Ellery was not present. That morning, Ellery was sent off to the station by his father in a melancholy mood, taking the 7:45 express train to leave New York. At the last moment, the young man changed his mind and announced that he would abandon his decision to travel to Maine and instead stay in New York to accompany his father until the case was closed. The old man disagreed. With his understanding of his son's character, he sensed how much the easily excited son longed for this holiday that had been coming for over a year. He didn't want to deprive his son of a pleasant journey just because of himself, so he was annoyed that his son wanted to stay by his side. [1977] He ignored Ellery's proposal, pushed him onto the train, and reluctantly smiled and patted him goodbye. As the train pulled out of the station, Ellery's last words came from the platform: \"I'll miss you, Dad. I'll write to you as soon as I can!\" [1978] The officer was now feeling the pain of separation from his son. He paced back and forth, fraying Pensler's carpet. He felt dizzy, weak, and nauseous, out of sync with the world and people, but he didn't hide his anger. [1979] \"It's about time, Pensler,\" he shouted at the short, stout manager, \"how long will it take to get rid of these damn audience?\" [1980] \"Soon, officer, very soon,\" Pensler replied. The district attorney blew his nose, which was running due to a cold. Juana stared in awe at his god in his mind. [1981] There was a knock on the door, and everyone turned their heads. The rumpled promoter Harry Nelson stuck his rough face into the room. \"Officer, mind if I join this little gathering?\" he asked cheerfully, \"I just started, is it about to end - oh, if you allow it, I'll stay here for a while!\" [1982] The officer gave him a cold glance from under his thick eyebrows. His posture was like Napoleon, his hair standing on end, his muscles bulging, and his face hostile. Sampson was surprised to see him. Quin had never shown his unexpected temperament. [1983] \"Well, it doesn't matter,\" he barked, \"there are enough people here anyway.\" [1984] Nelson's face turned red, and he seemed to want to leave. The officer blinked his eyes and felt better. [1985] \"Here - sit down, Nelson,\" he said politely, \"don't take it personally with an old man like me. I'm just a little tired. Tonight, I may need your help.\" [1986] \"I'd be delighted to join in, officer,\" Nelson grinned, \"What's this about \u2013 the Spanish Inquisition?\" [1987] \"Pretty much,\" the officer lowered his eyebrows, \"but - it depends on the situation.\" [1988] At this moment, the door opened and the towering and robust Sheriff Wiley strode into the room. He handed a piece of paper to the officer. [1989] \"Everyone's here, sir,\" he said. [1990] \"Has everyone gone out?\" he said loudly. [1991] \"Yes, sir. I sent the janitor down to the lounge, and they can't come back until we're done. The cashier has gone home, and so have the male and female ushers. The actors are backstage, I think they're changing clothes.\" [1992] \"Alright. Let's go, gentlemen,\" the officer strode out of the room, followed closely by Juno, who had been silent all evening, just quietly making approving noises. This scene was observed by the district attorney, who for some unknown reason was amused. [1993] The auditorium was once again empty, with rows of empty seats cold and vacant. The lights in the theater were all turned on, casting a dim glow over the front rows of the stalls. [1994] As the five men and Juno quickly made their way to the left aisle, someone's head bobbed in the left seating area. It was clear that a group of people were waiting for the officer's arrival. The officer slowly walked down the aisle, choosing a seat in front of the left box, so that everyone sitting could face him. Poulson, Nelson, and Sampson stood at one end of the aisle, while Juno watched excitedly by their side. [1995] This group of people was arranged in a peculiar way. The officer stood in the middle of the front row, and all the seats occupied from there to the back were directly next to the aisle. The last two rows of the twelfth row were filled with all kinds of people \u2013 men and women, young and old. These people had been sitting in the seats they were now occupying when the crime occurred, and the officer had personally checked them after finding the body. In the area of eight seats \u2013 Monty Field's seat and the surrounding empty seats \u2013 sat William Pusak, Esther Jablow, Maggie O'Connell, Jesse Lynch, and Johnny the Parson \u2013 \"the priest\" with a sneaky look, looking uneasy and covering his mouth with yellow-stained fingers, whispering to the usheress. [1996] The police officer suddenly made a gesture, and everyone fell silent. Sampson looked around at the chandeliers and lights, the empty theater, and the lowered curtain, feeling that the stage was set for the revelation of an exciting truth. He leaned forward with interest. Poulson and Nelson quietly observed the scene. Juno stared intently at the elderly man. [1997] \"Ladies and gentlemen,\" Quin looked at the group, getting straight to the point, \"I have brought you here for a specific purpose. I will not keep you here for longer than necessary. But what is necessary and what is not, is entirely up to me. If my questions do not receive the truthful answers I believe they should, then everyone will have to stay here until I am satisfied. I hope that before I continue, you all understand my meaning.\" [1998] He paused, looking around. There was a stir among the crowd, and suddenly there was a murmur, but it quickly subsided. [1999] \"On Monday evening,\" the police officer continued coldly, \"you all came to this theater to watch the performance, except for some employees and those sitting at the back. You are now sitting in the seats you were in that night.\" [2000] \"I want you to imagine that it is Monday evening now. I want you to remember that night, trying to recall everything that happened. I mean everything - any details, no matter how minor or seemingly insignificant, that left an impression in your memory...\"\n[2001] As the officer began to delve into the matter, several people entered the theater through the back door. Sampson greeted them quietly. Among this small group were Eve Ellis, Hilda Orlandi, Stephen Barry, James Pile, and several members of the \"Gunfight\" cast. They were all dressed casually. Pile whispered to Sampson that they had just come from the dressing room and had entered the audience section upon hearing the commotion. [2002] \"Quinn is in a meeting,\" Sampson whispered back. [2003] \"Will the officer object to us staying here to listen?\" Barry asked nervously, glancing at the officer, who stopped and stared coldly in their direction. [2004] \"I don't know...\" Sampson said worriedly, as Eve Ellis quietly shushed them, and they fell silent. [2005] \"Now -\" the officer said sternly after the commotion subsided, \"this is the situation. Remember, you are back to Monday evening. The second act's curtain has risen, and the lights have dimmed. There is commotion on stage, and you are all focused on the thrilling scene of the play... Did any of you, especially those sitting in the aisle seats, notice anything strange, unusual, or unsettling happening around or near you at that time?\" [2006] He paused expectantly. Everyone shook their heads blankly and fearfully. No one answered. [2007] \"Think hard,\" the officer said loudly, \"You remember that I asked you the same way along this aisle on Monday night. Of course, I don't expect you to lie, and if you can't remember what happened on Monday night, I don't have any reason to expect you to tell me anything amazing. But the situation is very urgent. Someone was murdered here, and frankly, we have to get to the bottom of it. It's one of our most challenging cases! We are blank and clueless - I am being open and honest with you, and I hope you will be too - given the situation, I must ask for your help. Only a few of you saw something important five days ago, and if something important comes up... based on my experience, people often forget some details under the pressure of tension and excitement, and after a few hours, days, or weeks, when they calm down, they gradually remember. I hope that happens to you...\"\n[2008] The officer's tone was sharp, and the people around him listened intently, momentarily forgetting their tension. After he finished speaking, people began to whisper, murmur, or occasionally shake their heads and quietly argue. The officer patiently waited. [2009] \"If there's anything to say, please raise your hand...\"\n[2010] A woman hesitated before raising her pale hand. [2011] \"Oh, Mrs.,\" Quin's finger pointed to her, \"Do you remember anything unusual?\" [2012] A frail old lady stood up nervously and spoke in a sharp, stammering voice: \"I don't know if it's important, sir,\" she said timidly, \"I remember at some point during the second act, a woman, I think a woman, walked down the corridor and then came back a few seconds later.\" [2013] \"Really? That's interesting, ma'am,\" the officer said. \"What time was that \u2013 do you remember?\" [2014] \"I don't remember the time, sir,\" the old lady said sharply. \"But it was probably around ten minutes after the curtain rose.\" [2015] \"I see\u2026 Do you remember what she looked like \u2013 young or old? What clothes was she wearing?\" [2016] The old lady looked puzzled. \"I'm not sure, sir,\" she quivered. \"I didn't notice \u2013\"\n[2017] A clear and loud voice came from the back row. Everyone turned around. Maggie O'Connell stood up. [2018] \"You don't have to bother with that, officer,\" she said coldly. \"The lady saw me walking up and down the aisle. You know what I'm talking about.\" She gave the officer a disdainful look. [2019] People gasped. The old lady looked uncertainly at the usher, then at the officer, and finally sat down slowly. [2020] \"That's not surprising,\" the officer said calmly. \"Well, are there any other witnesses?\" [2021] No one answered. Quinn realized that perhaps people didn't want to reveal their thoughts in front of others. So he walked down the aisle to each row, asking each person in a voice only they could hear. After asking everyone, he slowly returned to his seat. [2022] \"It seems I must let everyone return to their homes by the fireside,\" he said. \"Thank you for your help... adjourned!\" [2023] He said to them. The crowd stared at him, confused, and then whispered among themselves as they stood up, picked up their clothes and hats, and filed out of the theater under Wiley's stern gaze. Hilda Orland stood at the back of the crowd, sighing. [2024] \"I feel so sorry for the poor old man,\" she whispered to the others, \"Let's go, shall we?\" [2025] The actors mingled with the crowd as they left the theater. [2026] After the last person had disappeared, Constable Wiley turned back to the aisle, frowning and staring at the small group that remained. They seemed to feel the old man's anger and shivered, but the constable quickly changed his expression and became more approachable. [2027] He sat in a seat, crossing his hands behind his back and looking at Maggie O'Connell, Parson Johnny, and the others. [2028] \"Well, everyone,\" he said kindly, \"Parson, how are you? You're a free man, not bound by lawyers, so you can speak like any self-respecting citizen. Can you give us any help with this case?\" [2029] \"No,\" the little thug mumbled, \"I've already told you everything I know. There's nothing more to say.\" [2030] \"I understand... you know, Parson, we're very interested in your dealings with Mr. Field.\u201d The little thug looked up in shock. \"Yes,\" the constable continued, \"we want you to tell us about your past dealings with Mr. Field. Do you remember... Parson,\" he said sternly, \"who killed Monte Field? Who had a grudge against him? If you know - come clean!\" [2031] \"Oh, come on, officer,\" the \"priest\" grumbled, \"you're not going to pin it on me again? How would I know? Field was a slippery one\u2014he wouldn't go after his enemies. No, sir, I don't know...he was pretty decent to me\u2014helped me clear a few charges,\" he admitted brazenly, \"but I really\u2014just didn't know he was here Monday night.\" [2032] The officer turned to Maggie O'Connell. [2033] \"You, O'Connell?\" he said in a gentle tone, \"My son, Mr. Quinn, told me that Monday night you closed the exit door. You didn't tell me that. Do you know anything?\" [2034] The girl looked coldly at him. \"I've told you, officer, I have nothing more to say.\" [2035] \"You, William Pusak\u2014\" Quinn turned to the thin, diminutive bookkeeper, \"Do you remember anything you forgot about Monday night?\" [2036] Pusak shifted uncomfortably. \"I was just going to tell you, officer,\" he muttered, \"when I read about it in the paper, I remembered... on Monday night I leaned my head toward Mr. Field, and I smelled a very strong whiskey odor. I don't remember if I told you.\" [2037] \"Thank you,\" the officer said dryly, \"this is very helpful for our investigation. You can all go now...\"\n[2038] The orange juice vendor, Jesse Lynch, looked disappointed. \"You don't want to talk to me, sir?\" he asked anxiously. [2039] The officer smiled distractedly. \"Oh, yes. The helpful orange juice supplier...what do you have to say, Jesse?\" [2040] \"Oh, sir, this Field came to my stand to buy ginger ale before this, and I happened to notice him pick something up in the alley. It was a bit shiny, but I couldn't see too well. He put it right in the back pocket of his pants.\" [2041] He finished with a self-satisfied look, glancing around as if expecting applause. The officer seemed quite interested. [2042] \"What did that shiny thing look like, Jesse?\" he asked, \"Did it look like a gun?\" [2043] \"Gun? Oh, I don't think so,\" the boy selling orange juice expressed doubt, \"It was square, like... \"\n[2044] \"Like a woman's wallet?\" the officer interrupted. [2045] The boy's face lit up. \"Right!\" he exclaimed, \"I bet it's a wallet. Shiny all over, like a rainbow-colored stone.\" [2046] Quinn sighed. \"Well, Lynch, you're a good kid. You can go home now.\" [2047] The villains, the usher, Pascal, and his girlfriend, and the boy selling orange juice stood up silently and left. Wiley accompanied them to the door. [2048] Sampson waited until they had all left before pulling the officer aside. [2049] \"What's going on, Quinn?\" he asked, \"Is something not going well?\" [2050] \"Henry, old buddy,\" the officer smiled, \"We've done everything we can think of. We just need a little more time... I hope\u2014\" He didn't say what he hoped for.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 4:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:52 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nPusak stood up to squeeze past Field, and at that moment, Field was already poisoned. Pusak heard him murmur, \"Murder! Murdered!\" or something similar. [708] \"Pusak called a policeman, who then brought a doctor from the audience to check if Field was dead. The doctor confirmed that the victim had died from some kind of alcohol poisoning. Later, Assistant Coroner Plauti confirmed this, but he also added that there was an interference factor - usually, even if someone dies from alcohol poisoning, it won't happen so quickly. So, we can put aside the specific cause of death for now, as it can only be determined after autopsy. [709] \"Since there were many people in the audience and order needed to be maintained, the policeman called for help. The nearby police arrived to assist. They immediately launched an investigation. The first important question was whether the killer had the opportunity to leave the crime scene after the murder. Detective Doyle, who was the first to arrive at the crime scene, immediately ordered the theater manager to place people at every exit and both sides of the corridors to guard them. [710] \"When I arrived here, I immediately thought of this point and conducted a simple investigation myself. I circled all the exits and asked the people guarding them. I found that there was someone guarding every door in the theater during the first and second acts, of course, there were two exceptions that I will mention later. Now, through the boy who sells orange juice, Jesse Lynch, we can confirm that the victim was still alive not only during the intermission between the first and second acts - Lynch had seen him in the corridor and talked to him - but also about ten minutes after the second act began, when Lynch had delivered ginger ale to his seat, where he was later found dead. There is a usher standing under the stairs leading to the second floor in the theater, who swears that no one went up or down during the second act. This also rules out the possibility of the killer going upstairs. [711] \"The two exceptions I mentioned are the two doors at the leftmost aisle, which should have been guarded but were not, because the usher Maggie O'Connell was sitting next to her sweetheart at the time. This made me think of a possibility that the killer might have left through one of these two doors. If the killer was so talented, these two doors would be very convenient for escape. But the usher's testimony ruled out this possibility. This was something I discovered after my father asked her, and I followed up with more questions. [712] \"You snuck up on her, you little brat?\" Quinns glared at Ellery. \"I did talk to her,\" Ellery said with a smile. \"And I found an important fact that is closely related to our current investigation. O'Connell swore that she locked the door inside and out with the floor lock before leaving the two doors and sitting next to Johnny Parsen. When the commotion started, she immediately stood up from the 'priest' and ran to the door, only to find it still locked, just like when she left. So, she opened the door, and at that moment, Detective Dohal was trying to get the audience to quiet down. Unless she lied - but I don't think so. This also proves that the killer did not leave through these two doors, as the door was still locked from the inside when the body was discovered.\" [713] \"Well, I guess I'll have to change careers!\" Quinns grumbled. \"She didn't tell me anything about this, you damn woman! I'll deal with her later, this hateful woman!\" [714] \"Please be rational, Mr. Peacekeeper,\" Ellery said with a smile. \"She didn't tell you about locking the door because you didn't ask her. And she probably felt bad enough already.\" [715] \"The floor lock was a critical piece of evidence that helped us to narrow down the list of suspects to the two ushers, Maggie O'Connell and Jesse Lynch. However, the fact that the\n[716] \"Anyway, through her story, we can rule out the possibility that the killer escaped through the doors near the victim. I admit there are many possibilities - for example, Maggie O'Connell could be an accomplice. I'm just suggesting this as a possibility, not an opinion. At least in my opinion, the killer wouldn't take the risk of being seen leaving through those side doors. Moreover, leaving in an unusual way at an unusual time would be highly noticeable, especially during the second act when almost no one left. Besides, the killer couldn't have known that O'Connell would be neglecting her duties - unless she was an accomplice. Since this case was carefully planned - we must admit, the evidence suggests so - the killer might have given up the idea of escaping through the side doors.\" [717] \"The escape route, I think, is only one left, and that is the main entrance. However, the ticket taker and the doorkeeper outside confirmed that no one left the theater during the second act. Of course, except for the boy selling oranges, who was irrelevant.\" [718] \"All the exits were either guarded or locked, and from 9:35, there was someone in the alley - first Linnie, then Eleanor, then the male usher - Johnny Thurston - and then the police - these facts, along with my questioning and investigation, gentlemen. The inevitable conclusion is that the killer has been in the theater since the body was found!\" [719] After Ellery finished speaking, the room fell silent. \"By the way,\" Ellery added calmly, \"I remember asking the ushers if they saw anyone leave their seats during the second act, and they all said they couldn't remember anyone changing seats.\" [720] Quin absent-mindedly took a pinch of snuff. \"Well done - a reasonable deduction, my son. But ultimately, it's nothing surprising or decisive. Even if the killer was still in the theater, how can we catch him?\" [721] \"He didn't say you could catch him,\" said Sampson, smiling. \"Don't be so sensitive, old friend; nobody will say you were derelict in your duty during the investigation. As far as I know, you handled the whole thing very well tonight.\" [722] Quinn muttered, \"I admit, my not having thoroughly questioned the matter of the door bothers me a bit. But even if the murderer might have left immediately after the crime, I would still have asked the same question, hoping he would still be in the theater.\" [723] \"But, father\u2014of course!\" Ellery said solemnly, \"You have so many things to attend to, and my task is simply to sit back and observe, like Socrates.\" [724] \"So far, how have the people who have been surveyed been doing?\" Sampson curiously asked. [725] \"Hmm, how are they?\" Eleanor questioned, \"We can't seem to get a clear answer from either talking to them or observing their actions. We caught Parsons Johnny, the villain, but it's clear he was just a fan of the show, looking for some juicy gossip about his colleagues. As for Maggie O'Connell, she's suspicious, but we can't make any conclusions at this stage. She could be an accomplice, innocent, or just careless \u2013 she could play any role. Then there's William Pusack, who discovered Field. But have you noticed how slow-witted he is? And Benjamin Morgan \u2013 he's where we found the potential lead. But what can we make of his behavior tonight? Yes, the letter and the free ticket do sound odd, as anyone could have written that letter, even Morgan himself. I must also remember his public threat to kill Field; and the animosity between them, although the reason is unclear, has been going on for two years. Finally, there's Miss Frances Eves-Pope. Unfortunately, I wasn't present when you spoke with her. However, the fact remains that we found her handbag in the deceased's pocket \u2013 isn't that interesting? Could you explain that, please?\" [726] \"So far, that's the situation,\" Ellery continued regretfully. \"We have too many doubts from tonight's case, but too few facts.\" [727] \"So far, son,\" said Quin calmly. \"Your reasoning is flawless. But you've forgotten one important thing\u2014the empty seats. And another surprising fact\u2014the ticket stub of Field, and the only one that could possibly belong to the killer\u2014I mean the LL30 left one that Flint found\u2014these two tickets don't match. That is, the tear marks on the tickets show that the ticket taker checked these two tickets at different times!\" [728] \"Right,\" said Ellery. \"But let's put that aside for now and talk about Field's hat.\" [729] \"That hat\u2014uh, what do you think?\" Quin asked curiously. [730] \"Well, first, we're quite sure the hat didn't disappear by accident. Jesse Lynch saw the dead man's hat on his knees about ten minutes after the second act started. But now the hat is gone, and the only logical explanation is that the killer took it. Now\u2014no matter where the hat is\u2014we can immediately draw one of two conclusions: either the hat itself is connected to the case, and leaving it behind might reveal the killer's identity. We don't know what the connection is yet; or, the hat may contain something the killer wants. You might ask, why didn't the killer take what was inside and leave the hat behind? If this assumption is correct, it may be because he didn't have enough time to take it out, or he didn't know how to take it out, so he took the hat to study it later. Agree with my reasoning so far?\" [731] The district attorney nodded slowly. Quincannon sat motionless, a look of confusion in his eyes. [732] \"Now let's think about what might be in the hat. Ellery continued, wiping his glasses vigorously. \"Based on the size, shape, and volume of the hat, our range of possibilities is not very broad. What could be in a hat? Some of the things I can think of are mainly: some document, jewelry, money, or any other valuable small item that wouldn't be easily found in the hat. Obviously, this mysterious thing couldn't be in the top of the hat, because as soon as he took it off, it would fall out. So, we can conclude that whatever it is, it should be inside the hat. This immediately narrows down the possibilities. Solid large items can be ruled out. It could be jewelry, money, or documents. I think, through our understanding of Flint, we can rule out jewelry. If he carried something important, it would probably be related to his profession. [733] \"In the preliminary analysis of the missing hat, there is one more thing we need to consider. And, gentlemen, this should be crucial to our case before we solve it, that is, whether the killer knew before the murder that he had to take the hat away. In other words, did the killer know the importance of the hat, no matter how important it was? In my opinion, whether it's from the facts or from logical reasoning, it can be shown that the killer did not know the importance of the hat beforehand. [734] \"Please follow my line of thinking... Since Flint's hat is missing and no other hat was found in his place, it's clear that the hat was taken, and that's a fact. You should agree, as I pointed out earlier, that the killer is the most likely person to take the hat. Good! Let's put aside why he took the hat for now, we still have two choices: one, the killer knew beforehand that he had to take the hat; two, he didn't know beforehand that he had to take the hat. Let's examine the first possibility. If he knew beforehand, we can logically assume that the killer should have brought another hat to the theater to replace Flint's hat, instead of taking the victim's hat directly, leaving a clear clue. Bringing a replacement hat would be safer. And it shouldn't be difficult for the killer to find such a hat, because if he knew its importance beforehand, he would have further investigated the dimensions, style, and other details of Flint's hat. But we don't find a replacement hat here. We have every reason to believe that such a well-planned case should have had a replacement hat. But it's not there, so our only conclusion is that the killer didn't know the importance of the hat beforehand; otherwise, he would have taken preventive measures and left a hat. That way, the police would never know the importance of Flint's hat. [735] \"One more thing to confirm is that even if the killer had some unknown reason not to leave a replacement hat, he would have cut the hat open and\n[736] The district attorney stared at Ellery, his lips pressed together. Detective Quinn appeared to be dozing, his hand hovering between his snuff box and his nose. [737] \"What do you mean, Ellery?\" Sampson asked. \"Why is it so important that the killer didn't know the significance of the hat?\" [738] Ellery smiled. \"The murder occurred during the second act, and I wanted to use that to establish that if the killer didn't know the significance of the hat beforehand, he couldn't have planned the murder during the first intermission... Of course, Fielding's hat could appear somewhere in the theater, and once found, all these theories would be invalid. But... I don't think it will... \"\n[739] \"You've done some basic analysis, but it sounds logical,\" Sampson commended. \"You should become a lawyer.\" [740] \"You're no match for the Quinn family in terms of intellect,\" the old man suddenly broke into a grin. \"But I have to attend to something else related to the hat mystery. Ellery, have you noticed that the tailor's name is sewn onto Fielding's coat?\" [741] \"Let's do it,\" Ellery grinned, pulling out a small notebook from his coat pocket and flipping it open to a blank page. \"Brown Brothers, gentlemen\u2014that's it.\" [742] \"Alright, I'll have Wiley check it out first thing in the morning,\" Quine said. \"You must have realized that Mr. Field's clothing is of high quality. That evening suit would be worth around $300 if bought. Brown Bros. is a renowned tailor, so the price is high. Another relevant point is: all the clothes on the deceased were made by the same manufacturer. For wealthy people, this is nothing special; Brown's would make a complete outfit from head to toe to make the customer stand out. We can be more certain\u2014\"\n[743] \"Mr. Field's hat was also bought there!\" Sampson exclaimed, his face lighting up with a sense of discovery. [744] \"Absolutely correct, Tacitus,\" Quine said, grinning. \"Wiley's job is to verify the clothes, and if possible, get a replica of the same hat Mr. Field wore tonight. I'm eager to see this hat.\" [745] Sampson coughed and stood up. \"I think I should go back to bed,\" he said. \"The only reason I came here was to make sure you didn't arrest the mayor. Buddy, if my friend gets angry, my ears won't be quiet.\" [746] Quine looked up at him, smiling mockingly. \"Before you leave, Henry, tell me your position on this. I know I used high-pressure tactics tonight, but you must have realized it was necessary. Are you going to send someone from your side to assist in the investigation?\" [747] Sampson glared at him. \"When did you start feeling like I'm not satisfied with your investigation? You old fart!\" he roared. \"I've never questioned you, and I don't want to now. If you can't solve this, I don't think anyone else on my side can. My dear Quine, go ahead and do what you need to do. If you need to, you can detain half of New York, and I'll support you.\" [748] \"Thank you, Henry,\" Quine said. \"I just wanted to confirm. Now, since you're so nice, wait and see me do my thing.\" [749] He walked across the room, into the reception area, and called out to the theater from the corridor. \"Mr. Pensel, can you come here for a moment?\" [750] He came back, smiling slightly, as the dark-skinned Pensel followed behind him. [751] \"Mr. Poulson, this is District Attorney Sampson,\" Quinn introduced. They shook hands. \"Now, Mr. Poulson, you have one more thing to do before you can go rest. I want you to close the theater and not let any mice in.\" [752] Poulson's face paled. Sampson shrugged, as if he no longer cared about the matter, and Ellery nodded in agreement. [753] \"But\u2014but officer, we've been having a full house for our performances!\" the short manager moaned. \"Is it really necessary to do this?\" [754] \"Absolutely necessary, my dear fellow,\" the officer replied calmly. \"I'll have two men patrolling around the theater.\" [755] Poulson twisted his hands, casting a glance at Sampson. But the district attorney was turned away, admiring a painting on the wall. [756] \"Oh, that's terrible, Officer!\" Pincus sighed. \"Gordon Davis, the producer, will be on my case about this... But, of course\u2014if you say so, I'll do it.\" [757] \"Ah, don't be so glum, my friend,\" Quinn said, his tone more soothing. \"Through this, your theater's reputation will skyrocket. Once it reopens, you'll have to expand. Anyway, I won't keep your theater closed for too long. I'll give the necessary orders to my men outside, and you can quietly tell them what you need to do when you're done with your daily tasks tonight. You can go home after that. I'll tell you when the theater can reopen in a few days.\" [758] Pincus shook his head sadly and left after shaking hands with them. Sampson immediately turned to Quinn. \"My goodness, Quinn, that's a bit much! Why are you closing the theater? You've searched it top to bottom, haven't you?\" [759] \"But, Henry,\" Quinn said slowly, \"the hat is still missing. We've searched everyone who left the theater, and everyone has only one hat. That means the hat we're looking for must be somewhere here. If it is, I won't let anyone have the chance to come in and take it. I'll be the one to take it if it's here.\" [760] Sampson nodded. As the three of them left the office and entered the nearly empty theater, Ellery frowned with concern. Occasionally, a busy figure could be seen bending over the seats, carefully inspecting the ground. People kept coming and going from the front boxes. Chief Wiley stood by the main entrance, talking in a low voice with Pigott and Haggstrom. Detective Flint was directing some people to work in the front row of the orchestra. A group of tired janitors used vacuum cleaners to clean the theater. In a corner near the back, a plump female officer was talking to an elderly woman\u2014the same woman Pincus had referred to as Mrs. Philip. [761] Quinn and his companions approached the main entrance. Ellery and Sampson silently watched the empty theater, feeling the oppressive atmosphere. Quinn quickly spoke to Chief Inspector Wiley, whispering orders.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 5:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:52 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \n\"As far as I know, young man, he only has one. How many does a person need?\" [1615] \"You can be sure of that, I think,\" Ellery said. [1616] \"Absolutely, Mr. Quayle,\" she said in a sweet voice. Ellery stared at her as if she were a strange specimen. She pouted and turned away quickly. [1617] \"I'm not very popular here, so I'll be leaving... You're not going to put me in that disgusting jail, are you, officer? Can I go now?\" [1618] The officer bowed. \"Oh, yes\u2014you can go, Mrs. Rathbone, but under certain supervision... but in a few days, we'll need your charming company again, so I hope you'll understand. Can you stay in town?\" [1619] \"Of course, I promise!\" she laughed and strutted out of the room. [1620] Wiley jumped up like a soldier and said, \"Well, I guess that's settled, officer!\" [1621] The officer slumped into the chair, exhausted. \"Thomas, you're like a silly cop in Ellery's novel \u2014 of course, I mean no offense \u2014 suggesting that Morgan was arrested for the murder of Monte Field?\" [1622] \"How \u2014 could there be anything else?\" Wiley seemed bewildered. [1623] \"We'll have to wait a bit, Thomas,\" the old man replied gravely. [1624] Chapter 16: The Quinns Go to the Theater\n[1625] Ellery and his father sat across from each other in the small office. Wiley sat in the chair, frowning in silence for a moment before suddenly seeming to make up his mind to leave the room. [1626] The policeman toyed with the lid of a snuff box, grinning. [1627] \"You're surprised too, Ellery?\" [1628] But Ellery's expression was serious. \"The woman gave me a Woodhouse-style 'creepy feeling,' he said tremblingly. 'Scared' is too mild a word.\" [1629] \"I couldn't quite grasp the meaning of her attitude,\" Detective Quin said. \"I thought she knew, and we were just fumbling around... I was at a loss.\" [1630] \"I think this meeting was quite successful,\" Ellery commented. \"Mostly because I've been collecting interesting facts from this long-winded book about handwriting. But Mrs. Angela Rizzo doesn't fit my idea of the perfect woman...\"\n[1631] \"I'd say,\" chuckled the inspector, \"our fair friend has taken a shine to you. Seize the opportunity, son!\" [1632] Ellery made a face of distaste. [1633] \"All right!\" Inspector Queer picked up the telephone on the desk. \"You think we should give Benjamin Morgan another chance, Ellery?\" [1634] \"He's not worth it,\" Ellery grumbled. \"But it's routine.\" [1635] \"You forgot the documents, son\u2014the documents.\" The inspector's eyes twinkled. [1636] He spoke to the operator at the police station in a cheerful tone, and after a while the phone rang. [1637] \"Good afternoon, Mr. Morgan!\" Quin said jovially. \"How are you today?\" [1638] \"Is this Inspector Quin?\" Morgan hesitated slightly. \"Good afternoon, sir. Has there been any progress in the case?\" [1639] \"That's a good question, Mr. Morgan,\" the policeman laughed. \"But I'm afraid I can't answer that yet, I'm afraid I might be accused of incompetence... Mr. Morgan, are you free tonight?\" [1640] There was a pause on the other end. \"Well\u2014to be exact, no. I'm going home, of course, for dinner, and I believe my wife has arranged for bridge. Anything, Inspector?\" [1641] \"I was hoping to invite you and my son to dinner tonight,\" the officer said regretfully, \"Can you come out for dinner?\" [1642] There was a longer pause. \"If it's absolutely necessary, officer...\"\n[1643] \"It's not absolutely necessary, Mr. Morgan... If you accept the invitation, I would be very grateful.\" [1644] \"Oh.\" Now Morgan's voice was more firm, \"Well, then I'll do as you say, officer. Where shall we meet?\" [1645] \"Great, great!\" Quinn said, \"Six o'clock at Carlos Restaurant, how about that?\" [1646] \"Very well, officer.\" The lawyer replied calmly and hung up the phone. [1647] \"I can't help feeling sorry for the guy,\" the old man murmured. [1648] Ellery muttered incoherently, without sympathy. Angela Lashaw's influence was a thorn in his side. [1649] At exactly six o'clock, Quin and Ellery met Benjamin Morgan in the cozy lobby of Carlos Restaurant. He sat dejectedly in a red leather chair, staring at his hand; his lips drooped with worry, and his legs were subconsciously spread apart due to his gloomy mood. [1650] As the Quin father and son approached, he forced a smile and stood up firmly. This gesture conveyed his determination to the two kind-hearted hosts. The officer was in high spirits, partly because he really liked the plump lawyer and partly because of the official business. Ellery was, as usual, ambiguous in his attitude. [1651] The three men shook hands like old friends. [1652] \"I'm glad you could make it on time, Morgan,\" said the inspector. [1653] A reserved waiter led them to a table in the corner. \"I'm very sorry to pull you away from your meal. Once upon a time\u2014\" he sighed, and everyone sat down. [1654] \"No need to apologize,\" Morgan smiled weakly. \"I suppose you both know that every married man occasionally wishes to attend a bachelor's party\u2014like now. Inspector, do you have something to discuss with me?\" [1655] The old man raised his finger and made a warning gesture. \"Not now, Morgan,\" he said. \"I have an idea: Louis has a talent for cuisine\u2014right, Louis?\" [1656] Dinner was a feast of culinary delights. The officer paid no heed to the subtleties of cooking and handed the menu to his son. Ellery had always been fascinated by food and its preparation. The three enjoyed the meal immensely. Morgan initially tasted the food absentmindedly but grew to appreciate the delicious dishes in front of him, eventually forgetting his worries and engaging in lively conversation with the hosts. [1657] They sipped milk coffee, and Ellery carefully smoked a fine cigar. The officer steered clear of cigars, while Morgan eagerly smoked one. Quin then cut to the chase. [1658] \"Morgan, I don't want to beat around the bush. I'm sure you know why I invited you here tonight. I'll get straight to the point. I want you to explain why you haven't mentioned the events of four days ago, on Sunday, September 23rd.\" [1659] At the officer's words, Morgan's expression grew grave. He placed his cigar in the ashtray and stared at the old man with an indescribable weariness. [1660] \"The inevitable always comes,\" he said. \"I knew you'd eventually find out. I suppose Mrs. Rathbone told you everything in her anger.\" [1661] \"Yes,\" Quin admitted candidly. \"As a man of status, I wouldn't listen to rumors; but as a police officer, it's my duty. Why did you keep it a secret, Morgan?\" [1662] Morgan drew a meaningless pattern on the tablecloth with his spoon. \"Because...uh, because people are idiots unless someone tells them how stupid they are.\" He looked up calmly and said, \"I had hoped and prayed\u2014it's a weakness of human nature, I suppose\u2014that this would be a secret between me and the dead. Discovering that whore hiding in that bedroom\u2014hearing every word I said\u2014made me feel so humiliated.\" [1663] He took a big gulp of water and continued, \"Inspector, I thought I had been set up and couldn't provide any favorable evidence. It was true. I found my most hated enemy murdered at the theater, and it happened just a few steps away from me. I couldn't explain why I was at the scene of the crime unless I told a ridiculous, baseless story; in that moment of agony, I remembered that I had argued with the victim the night before.\" [1664] The inspector remained silent. Ellery leaned back in his chair, frowning at Morgan. Morgan swallowed hard and continued. [1665] \"That's why I didn't say anything. Can you blame someone who, when the law clearly warns him that he is digging his own trap, chooses to remain silent?\" [1666] Quinn paused for a moment before saying, \"Let's not talk about that, Morgan. Why did you go to Field's on Sunday night?\" [1667] \"For a good reason,\" the lawyer said painfully, \"Last Thursday, Field came to my office and told me he needed to make a final business investment and needed to find a way to get fifty thousand dollars immediately. Fifty thousand dollars!\" [1668] \"This is very interesting, Mr. Morgan,\" Ellery said, his eyes flashing. \"Which sentence in the conversation made you suspect he was 'settling old scores'?\" [1669] \"Yes, that's why I said it. He gave the impression that he was short of money and wanted to take a vacation\u2014a trip to Europe for at least three years\u2014he was soliciting funds from 'friends.' I didn't know he was blackmailing on a large scale, but this time\u2014\"\n[1670] Ellery and the inspector exchanged glances. Morgan continued speaking. [1671] \"I told him the truth: I'm not in a good financial situation right now, mainly because of him, and I certainly can't come up with the absurd amount he asked for. He just smiled - insisted on the money. Of course, I was eager to get those documents back...\"\n[1672] \"Did you check the canceled checks to see if those documents were actually lost?\" the officer asked. [1673] \"No, officer,\" Morgan said angrily, \"Actually, he showed me those documents and letters two years ago at the Webster Club - we had an argument. Oh, they're fine. He's a man of his word.\" [1674] \"Go on.\" [1675] \"Last Thursday, he openly threatened me and hung up the phone. In the conversation, I tried to convince him that I would find a way to meet his demands, because I knew that once he realized he had squeezed me dry, he would expose those documents without hesitation...\"\n[1676] \"Did you ask him to show you the documents?\" asked Ellery. [1677] \"I think I did - but he laughed and said he would show me the invoices and letters when he saw the money. He's sly, that con artist - he wouldn't risk me killing him while he was pulling out that damning evidence\u2026 You see, I'm being so honest. I can even admit that the thought of violence occasionally crosses my mind. In that situation, who wouldn't think that? But I never really wanted to kill anyone - there's a good reason for that.\" [1678] \"That doesn't benefit you at all,\" Ellery whispered. \"You still don't know where the documents are!\" [1679] \"That's right,\" Morgan nervously laughed. \"I don't know. Those documents could be exposed at any time - fall into someone else's hands - what good would it do for me if Field died? I might just be trading one bad foreman for another\u2026 On Sunday night, I managed to scrape together money for three days, but to no avail - I decided to come to a final reckoning with him. I went to his apartment, found him in his pajamas, very surprised to see me, but without fear. The living room was a mess - I didn't know Mrs. Rask was hiding in the next room.\" [1680] He lit a cigar with trembling fingers. [1681] \"We argued - to be precise, I argued, and he sneered. He didn't care about any reasons or pleas. He wanted the fifty thousand dollars, or he'd expose the details... and the evidence. After a while, I was furious... I left before I lost control. That's it, officer, I swear it on my honor as a gentleman and an unfortunate victim of fate.\" [1682] He turned his face away. Inspector Quin coughed and threw his cigar into the ashtray. He took a brown snuff box from his pocket, took a pinch, took a deep breath, and leaned back in his chair. Ellery suddenly poured Morgan a glass of water, which he drank in one gulp. [1683] \"Thank you, Morgan,\" Quin said, \"since you've been so candid about the details, would you please tell me honestly if you threatened Field's life during your argument on Sunday night. For fairness' sake, I must tell you that Mrs. Rathbone firmly accused you of killing Field.\" [1684] Morgan turned pale, his eyebrows twitched, and his eyes grew vacant and anxious as he stared pitifully at the officer. [1685] \"She's lying!\" he croaked. Several diners at nearby tables looked around curiously, and Inspector Quin patted Morgan's arm. Morgan bit his lip and whispered. \"I didn't do that, officer. I just told you the truth. I had cruel thoughts of killing Field. It was an impotent, stupid, meaningless idea, and I - I didn't have the guts to kill him. Even when I threatened him in the club, I didn't mean it. Of course, on Sunday night - please believe me, don't believe that shameless, money-hungry whore, officer - you have to believe me!\" [1686] \"I just want you to explain what you said. Because,\" Detective Quin said calmly, \"although it seems strange, I do believe you said what she thought you said.\" [1687] \"What did she say?\" Morgan sweat, his eyes bulging. [1688] \"\u2018Expose those documents if it means destroying me - I guarantee it's your last time blackmailing someone!'\" Detective Quin answered, \"Did you say that, Morgan?\" [1689] The lawyer stared incredulously at the Quin father and son, then burst out laughing. \"Oh my god!\" he gasped, \"That's what she thought was a threat? Officer, I meant that if I couldn't meet his extortionate demands, I would turn myself in to the police and drag him down with me. That's what I meant! She thought I was threatening his life -\"\n[1690] Ellery chuckled, motioned for the waiter to come over, paid the bill, and lit a cigarette, glancing sideways at his father. Detective Quin sympathetically and blankly stared at Morgan. [1691] \"Very well, Mr. Morgan,\" the officer stood up, pushing his chair back, \"That's all we wanted to know.\" He politely turned to the side, allowing the dazed and trembling lawyer to exit first, heading towards the cloakroom. [1692] As the Quinn father and sons strolled down Broadway along Forty-Seventh Street, the sidewalk in front of the Roman Theater was packed with people. The crowd surged, and a barricade was set up to maintain order. The entire narrow passage was at a standstill. The strong lights above the marquee flashed the play's title, \"The Gunfight,\" with smaller lights illuminating the cast list: starring James Parrish, Eve Ellis, and a full cast of stars. Men and women elbowed their way through the throng, police shouted for people to show their tickets to pass the barricade. [1693] The officer flashed his badge, and he and Ellery were pushed and shoved along with the throng of spectators into the theater's small foyer. The manager, Poulson, stood beside the ticket booth with a smile on his Latin American face; he was polite, firm, and authoritative, helping customers move quickly from the ticket booth to the ticket check. The doorman, covered in sweat, stood by, looking bewildered. The cashier worked diligently. Harry Nelson was cornered, engaged in intense conversation with three young men who appeared to be reporters. [1694] Poulson spotted the Quinn father and sons and approached them. Seeing the officer's proud demeanor, he hesitated, nodding knowingly, and returned to the cash window. Ellery stood in line, obtaining two reserved tickets from the ticket booth. They wove through the throng of people, making their way to the front row. [1695] When Ellery handed Maggie O'Connell two tickets clearly marked LL32 Left and LL30 Left, she took a step back in surprise. She awkwardly took the tickets, glancing nervously at the officer, who smiled. She led them down the left aisle to the back row, pointing to the last two seats, and quickly left. They sat down, hanging their hats on the metal racks under the seats, and comfortably leaned back, appearing to be two pleasure-seekers, eagerly anticipating the evening's violent and bloody performance. [1696] The auditorium was packed. Crowds of people lined the aisles, being ushered quickly to their seats by ushers. Many people turned to look at the Quin father and son, who had inadvertently become the center of attention. [1697] \"Damn it!\" the older man complained, \"We should have waited until the curtain went up.\" [1698] \"You're too sensitive to public acclaim, Father,\" Ellery laughed. \"I don't mind being the center of attention.\" He glanced at his watch, and the two exchanged meaningful glances. It was 8:25. They shifted in their seats and settled in. [1699] One by one, the lights went out. The chatter of the audience quieted down. In the darkness, the curtain rose on the stage, revealing a mysterious scene. A gunshot broke the silence; a man let out a gurgling cry, and the audience gasped. \"The Gunfight\" opened in a well-known, exaggerated manner. [1700] Compared to his father's intense concentration, Ellery sat back in his chair, relaxed, and enjoyed the excellent melodrama. James Pierce's deep, resonant voice echoed through the stage, captivating him with the dramatic narrative. Eve Ellis' focus on her role was evident \u2013 she was currently speaking in a low, quivering voice with Stephen Barry. Barry's handsome appearance and pleasing voice prompted the young girl sitting next to the officer to exclaim in admiration. Hilda O'Lanci was squeezed into a corner, dressed in flashy attire, fitting her stage persona. The elderly \"character actor\" aimlessly wandered around the stage. Ellery leaned towards his father. [1701] \"This is a strong cast,\" he whispered. \"Look at that O'Loughlin woman!\" [1702] The performance was full of twists and turns, punctuated by the occasional sound effect, accompanied by the symphony of dialogue and noise. The first act ended. The lights came on, and the detective glanced at his watch: 9:05. [1703] He stood up, and Ellery lazily followed. Marcia O'Connell pretended not to see them, pushing the heavy iron door opposite the aisle. The audience poured out into the dimly lit alley.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 6:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:52 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \n\"I'll keep it to myself, believe me!\" [1861] \"Okay,\" Quin stroked the lining of the hat, which made a clear crackling sound. [1862] \"Now,\" Ellery said calmly, \"we have our first clear indication of why the killer had to take Field's hat on Monday night. The killer's name is likely to be written in the same way - it's an indelible ink, you know - the killer wouldn't leave a hat with his name on it at the crime scene.\" [1863] \"If you find that hat,\" Corning called out, \"you'll know who the killer is!\" [1864] \"Tim, I'm afraid,\" the officer replied calmly, \"that the hat is gone forever.\" [1865] He pointed to the bottom of the inner band connected to the lining. He quickly pulled off those stitches, stuck his fingers into the lining and the hat top, and then silently pulled out a bundle of documents tied together with a thin rubber band. [1866] \"If I were as disliked as some people think,\" Ellery said, leaning back and thinking aloud, \"I would say with complete fairness: 'I told you so.'\" [1867] \"We know when to expect a hit from you, son - don't repeat yourself,\" the officer chuckled, breaking the rubber band and quickly scanning the papers, smiling in satisfaction, and putting them in his jacket pocket. [1868] \"Morgan's, right,\" he said briefly, and began to examine another round-top hat. The inside of the sweatband had a vague X marked on it. The officer found a row of stitches similar to those in the silk hat lining. He pulled out the file - thicker than Morgan's bundle - and curiously checked it. He then handed it to Cronin, who's fingers were trembling. [1869] \"Lucky for you, Tim,\" he said slowly, \"the person you're tracking is dead, but this involves a lot of high-profile people. I think you'll find yourself a hero in a few days.\" [1870] Cronin grabbed the bundle of papers, excitedly spreading them out. \"They're here - they're here!\" he exclaimed, stuffing the stack of papers into his pocket. [1871] \"I have to go, officer,\" he said urgently. \"There's a lot of work to do \u2013 and what you found in the fourth hat is none of my business. I'm grateful to you and Mr. Quinn! Goodbye!\" [1872] He rushed out of the room, and a moment later, the snoring of the policeman in the hallway stopped. The outside door slammed shut. [1873] Ellery and the officer exchanged glances. [1874] \"I don't see how these things are of any use to us,\" the old man muttered, running his fingers over the sweatband of the last top hat. \"We've discovered things and made deductions at a much faster pace than we anticipated \u2013 oh well...\" He sighed, holding the band up to the light. [1875] It read: Miscellaneous. [1876] Chapter 18: The Standoff\n[1877] On Friday afternoon, while Inspector Quin, Ellery, and Tim Cronin were searching the Montague Field house, Officer Wiley, as usual, walked slowly up the brownstone steps of the Quin residence from Broadway to Eighty-seventh Street, rang the bell. Juno answered with a cheerful voice, and the officer came up sternly. [1878] \"The officer's not home!\" Juno announced impulsively, her thin body completely hidden behind the large apron. The smell of onion-fried steak filled the air. [1879] \"Get out of here, kid!\" Wiley shouted. He pulled out a sealed envelope from his chest pocket and handed it to Juno, \"Give it to the officer when he gets back. If you forget, I'll dump you in the East River.\" [1880] \"And who else?\" Juno whispered, his lips trembling, and then he politely added, \"Yes, sir.\" [1881] \"Well, then.\" Wiley turned calmly and went downstairs to the street, his broad back very visible, in stark contrast to Juno's grin in the window on the fourth floor. [1882] Around six o'clock, the two Quinns returned to their residence, and Officer Wiley suddenly noticed the official envelope lying on his plate. [1883] He tore the corner of the envelope and pulled out some documents typed on police stationery. [1884] \"Oh, oh!\" He murmured to the lazily disrobing Ellery. \"The gang is gathering...\"\n[1885] He sat down in the armchair, leaving his hat on and his coat buttoned, and began to read the report. [1886] The first one read:\n[1887] Release Report\n[1888] (September 28, 1920)\n[1889] John Casanelli, also known as Johnny Parsons, alias \"Johnny North Italian,\" also known as Peter Dominic, was granted parole today. [1890] The investigation into John's accomplice in the robbery incident, the Bonomo Silk Robbery (June 2, 1920), has not yielded any further information. We are investigating the informant \"Lovable\" Morehouse for further information, who has disappeared from his usual haunts. [1891] Parole was granted to Jo Casanelli at the suggestion of District Attorney Sampson, but he is under supervision and must be available at a moment's notice. [1892] T. O'W. [1893] The police officer raised his eyebrows and set aside the information about Parson Johnny, picking up the second report which read:\n[1894] Report on William Pusack\n[1895] (September 28, 19XX)\n[1896] An investigation into William Pozsak's history revealed the following:\n[1897] Thirty-two years old; born in Brooklyn, New York, with parents naturalized; unmarried; normal habits; enjoys socializing; has \"dates\" three to four nights a week; religious. [1898] Since Monday evening, his activities have been normal. No letters sent out, no withdrawals from the bank, and his life has been quite normal. No suspicious behavior. [1899] Esther Jablow, a girl who seems to be Pozsak's \"most stable companion,\" has seen him twice since Monday: during lunchtime on Tuesday and Wednesday evening. On Wednesday evening, they went to the movies and a Chinese restaurant. [1900] The fourth informant\n[1901] (T. Weir: Agreed)\n[1902] The officer snorted and tossed the paper aside. The third report is titled:\n[1903] Report on Maggie O'Connell\n[1904] (September 28, 1920)\n[1905] O'Connell lives at 1436 Tenth Avenue. Renting an apartment on the fourth floor. No father. Since Monday evening, she has been idle due to the closure of the Roman Theater. She left the theater with the audience on Monday evening. She went home but stopped at a pharmacy on Eighth Avenue and made a call on Forty-eighth Street. The call could not be traced. Overheard conversation on the phone involved Johnny Parsons. She seemed excited. [1906] She did not leave the house until 1 pm on Tuesday. She did not contact Parson Johnny in Topeka. After discovering that the Roman Theater was indefinitely closed, she went to a nearby employment agency to seek a usher's position. [1907] There were no new developments on Wednesday and Thursday. She returned to work at the Roman Theater on Thursday evening after receiving a call from the manager. She did not meet or contact Parson Johnny. There were no incoming calls, no visitors, and no letters. She seemed suspicious \u2013 she \"knew\" she was being followed. [1908] The Eleventh Detective\n[1909] (Tommy Weir: Agreed)\n[1910] \"Hmm!\" the officer muttered, picking up the next report. \"Let's see what this one says...\"\n[1911] Report on Francis Evans-Pope\n[1912] (September 28, 19XX)\n[1913] After being released from the manager's office at the Roman Theater on Monday evening by Inspector Quin, Francis Evans-Pope immediately left the theater. She was checked along with other audience members at the main entrance. She left with Eve Ellis, Stephen Barry, Hilda Orrance, and other actors. They took a taxi to Evans-Pope's residence on the riverside. She was in a semi-conscious state when she was taken out. The three actors left the house afterward. [1914] She didn't leave the house on Tuesday. The gardener informed her that she spent the whole day in bed. She received many phone calls that day. [1915] She didn't officially appear until Wednesday morning when she met Inspector Quin at her residence. After the meeting, she left the house with Stephen Barry, Eve Ellis, James Pear, and her brother Stanford. Evans-Pope drove them in a luxury car to Westchester. The outing revitalized her. She spent the evening at home with Stephen Barry, playing bridge. [1916] On Thursday, she went shopping on Fifth Avenue and had lunch with Stephen Barry. He took her to Central Park, and they spent the afternoon outdoors. Barry escorted her home before 5 PM. He stayed for dinner, and after dinner, he received a call from the stage manager to return to the Roman Theater. Ives-Pope spent the evening at home with her family. [1917] There was no report on Friday morning. There were no suspicious behaviors throughout the week. She did not speak to any strangers. She did not contact Benjamin Morgan. [1918] The 39th Informant\n[1919] (Agreed by T. Wharton)\n[1920] \"That's all,\" the officer said quietly. He selected the next report, which was quite short. [1921] Report on Oscar Lourne\n[1922] (September 28, 19XX)\n[1923] Lourne worked with Mr. Stoutz and Mr. Croning in his office at Monte Field on Tuesday, Wednesday, Thursday, and Friday mornings. The three had lunch together every day. Lourne is married and resides at 211 Sixteenth Street in the Bronx. He spends his evenings at home. There are no suspicious letters or phone calls. No bad habits. His life is strict and modest. His reputation is good. [1924] 16th Street Informant\n[1925] Note: Detailed information about Oscar Lourne's background and habits can be obtained through Assistant District Attorney Timothy Croning. [1926] (T. W.: Agreed)\n[1927] The policeman sighed and put five files into a plate, stood up, took off his hat and coat, and threw them with a jerk into the hands of the waiting Juno. He then picked up the last report from the envelope - a large sheet of paper held together with a pin, with a small note on it: \"Memo to L. Quill.\" [1928] The note read:\n[1929] This morning Dr. Plaut sent me the attached report to deliver to you. He regrets that he cannot personally report to you, but the Brubling poison case has taken up all his time. [1930] The signature was the familiar and careless initials of a name that Willy knew. [1931] The attachment was a hastily typed brief message, with the heading of the medical office. [1932] Dear Quer: This is about the poison tetraethyl lead. Jones and I have investigated all possible sources of transmission, but without success. I believe you are just going to have to rely on luck in this matter. You will never trace the poison that killed Monte Field. This is not only my humble opinion, but also that of the chief and Jones. We all agree that the most logical explanation is gasoline. Try to find that thing, detective! [1933] Dr. Plauti's postscript read:\n[1934] Of course, I will notify you immediately if anything is found. Stay alert. [1935] \"Useless!\" the policeman muttered, while Ellery silently ate the delicious meal prepared by Zunna. The policeman poked at the fruit salad, looking very unhappy. He grumbled quietly, glanced at the report on the plate with annoyance, stared at Ellery's tired face and his chin devouring the food, and finally threw away the spoon. [1936] \"In all the useless, exasperating, empty reports I've ever seen\u2014\" he roared. [1937] Ellery smiled. \"Of course, do you remember Perianthus? \u2026 Ah? You may be polite, sir \u2026 Perianthus of Collins Bay, who said in his lucid moments, 'Diligence can do anything!'\" [1938] The fire was roaring, and Jones curled up in his favorite corner on the floor. Ellery smoked, comfortably gazing at the flames, while old Quin filled his nose with snuff. The two Quins settled down to a serious discussion. More precisely \u2013 Quin Inspector settled down, talking seriously to his son, as Ellery seemed to be in an extremely dreamy mood, far from the unpleasant details of crime and punishment. [1939] The old man slapped his hand on the armrest of the chair. \"Ellery, have you ever seen such a perplexing case in your life?\" [1940] \"On the contrary,\" Ellery commented, half-closing his eyes and staring at the fire, \"it's quite natural for you to be nervous. You're getting worked up over something as trivial as arresting the killer. Please forgive my hedonistic philosophy \u2026 if you recall, in my story called 'The Black Window Incident,' the detective I wrote about had no difficulty capturing the criminals. Why? Because they stayed calm. Conclusion: always stay calm \u2026 I'm thinking about tomorrow. A pleasant holiday!\" [1941] \"For a gentleman, son,\" the officer barked angrily, \"your lack of coherence is surprising. Your words mean nothing, and your silence speaks volumes. No, I'm confused... \"\n[1942] Ellery burst into laughter. \"Maine's forests - brown - lakeside charming cabins - fishing rods - air - oh, Lord, will tomorrow ever come?\" [1943] The elder Quine looked at his son with a mixture of pity and warmth. \"I... I hope... Hmm, don't mind. I mean, Ellery, if my little thief fails... Then it's all over.\" [1944] \"Damn thief!\" Ellery exclaimed. \"What does Pon have to do with human suffering? My next book is almost finished, dad.\" [1945] \"Stealing inspiration from real life, you scoundrel,\" the old man muttered, \"if you use the case of Field as a plot, I would be very interested in reading the last few chapters you wrote!\" [1946] \"Poor Dad!\" Ellery chuckled, \"Don't take life so seriously. Fail and fail, as they say. Anyway, Monte Field wasn't worth a dime.\" [1947] \"That's not the point,\" the old man said, \"I don't want to admit defeat\u2026 The motives and conspiracies in this case are tangled like a ball of yarn, Ellery. In my entire career, I've never encountered such a tough nut to crack. It's enough to give you a stroke! I know who killed the person\u2014I know why they did it, and I even know how they did it! But where am I?\u201d He paused, took out a pinch of snuff, \"I'm in the middle of nowhere, right there!\u201d He roared, then gradually calmed down. [1948] \"Certainly a very unusual situation,\" Ellery murmured, \"However, the more difficult problem has been solved\u2026 Hey! I can't wait to bathe in the idyllic stream!\" [1949] \"You might get pneumonia,\" the officer said worriedly, \"Now promise me, young man, that you won't do any of those silly things that go against nature while you're there. I don't want to plan a funeral\u2014I\u2026\"\n[1950] Ellery suddenly fell silent, looking at his father. The officer's face seemed unusually aged under the flickering light of the fire, and the pain on his face made his deep wrinkles more human. His hands looked fragile and worried, gently brushing back his thick gray hair. [1951] Ellery stood up, blushing and hesitated for a moment before quickly bending forward and patting his father's shoulder. [1952] \"Cheer up, dad,\" he whispered. \"If it weren't for Sandford and me arranging things... everything would be fine\u2014believe me. If there's any way I can help you, even a little... but there isn't. It's your job now, dad, and no one can handle this problem better than you in the world...\"\n[1953] He disappeared into the bedroom. Juno sat in the corner in a Turkish manner, quickly getting up and walking across the room to the officer's chair. He sat on the floor, leaning his head against the old man's knees. The silence was occasionally broken by the crackling of wood in the fireplace and the dull sound of Ellery moving around in the next room. [1954] Inspector Quin was tired. His face was pale, gaunt, and wrinkled, looking like a relief in the dim red light. He stroked Juno's curly hair. [1955] \"Juno, boy,\" he whispered, \"don't become a policeman when you grow up.\" [1956] Junna turned his head, looking solemnly at the old man. \"I want to be like you,\" he announced. [1957] The phone rang, and the old man jumped up. He grabbed the phone from the table, his face pale and his voice choked. \"This is Quin,\" he said. [1958] After a moment, he put down the phone and staggered towards the bedroom. He leaned heavily against the doorframe. Ellery straightened up from his suitcase - and jumped forward. [1959] \"Dad!\" he cried, \"What's wrong?\" [1960] The inspector tried to smile. \"It's just... a little tired, son, I think,\" he grunted, \"I just got a tip from a small-time crook... \"\n[1961] \"That-\"\n[1962] \"He found nothing.\" [1963] Ellery took his father's arm and led him to a chair by the bedside. The old man slumped into the chair, his eyes tired and weary. \"Ellery, son,\" he said. \"The last piece of evidence is gone. It's enough to drive you crazy! There's no solid evidence in court to prove the killer guilty. What do we have? A series of perfect reasoning - that's all. A good lawyer can find loopholes in the case... Oh! It's not over yet.\" [1964] He got up from the chair, suddenly serious. He patted Ellery's broad back with vigor. \"Go to bed, son,\" he said. \"You have to get up early tomorrow. I'll stay up and think about it.\" [1965] Interlude\n[1966] Please note\n[1967] The current detective novel craze places the reader in the position of the chief detective. I convinced Ellery Queen to allow me to add a challenge to the reader in \"The Roman Hat Mystery\" \u2013 \"Who killed Monte Field?\"\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 7:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:52 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nIt was just a matter of painting a little on a whole piece of healthy skin - I'll tell you, I was shocked. If there were a wound on the skin or if it was ingested internally, that would be another matter. So, you can imagine what would happen to Field after swallowing this stuff - he drank a lot of it!\" [1335] Ellery's eyebrows furrowed in thought. He began to wipe the lenses of his spectacles. [1336] \"This is not all,\" Dr. Jones continued. \"As far as I know \u2013 I've been working in this city for years and I have some knowledge of developments in other parts of the world \u2013 but as far as I know, tetraethyl lead has never been used for criminal purposes.\" [1337] The detective sat up straight, his eyes widening. \"That's significant, doctor!\" he murmured. \"Are you sure?\" [1338] \"Of course. That's why I'm so interested.\" [1339] \"How long would it take to kill someone with this poison, doctor?\" Ellery asked slowly. [1340] Dr. Jones looked grim. \"I can't give you an exact answer because, as far as I know, no one has died from this poison before. But I can make a fairly accurate guess. I think Field would not have lasted more than fifteen minutes, maybe twenty, after ingesting this poison.\" [1341] Quin's cough broke the ensuing silence. \"On the other hand, doctor,\" he said, \"isn't the uniqueness of this poison what makes it easy to trace? What do you think is its most common source? Where does it come from? If I wanted to use it for criminal purposes without leaving a trace, how would I get it?\" [1342] The toxicologist's face twisted into a bitter smile. \"Tracking down this poison,\" he said earnestly, \"is up to you. You can look it up. What I can confirm right now - remember, it's almost completely new to us - tetraethyl lead is most commonly found in a certain type of petroleum product. I stumbled upon the simplest method to produce it in large quantities. You won't guess how. It can be extracted from the most common and ordinary gasoline.\" [1343] The Quin father and son exclaimed in a low voice, \"Gasoline!\" The detective shouted, \"Ah - but how are we supposed to trace it?\" [1344] \"The problem is here,\" the toxicologist replied. \"I can go to a gas station on the corner, fill up a tank, drive home, and extract tetraethyl lead from the gas tank with a little time and effort.\" [1345] \"Does that mean, doctor,\" Ellery interjected hopefully, \"that the killer needs some experience with chemical experiments - familiarity with chemical analysis, or something like that?\" [1346] \"It doesn't say that. Anyone with a home distillation apparatus can extract tetraethyl lead, and leave no trace. The advantage of the process is that the boiling point of tetraethyl lead is higher than any other component in gasoline. All you have to do is extract the other components at a certain temperature, and what's left is this poison.\" [1347] The officer took a pinch of snuff and his fingers trembled slightly. \"I can only say \u2013 I'm in awe of this killer,\" he muttered. \"Tell me, Doctor, does one need to have knowledge of toxicology to know this? If he doesn't have a particular interest in this area \u2013 and hasn't been trained in it \u2013 how could he know this?\" [1348] Dr. Jones snorted. \"Officer, you surprise me. Your question has been answered already.\" [1349] \"What's going on? What do you mean?\" [1350] \"I just told you how to extract it, didn't I? If you heard this from a toxicologist, and had a distillation room, wouldn't you be able to extract some too? You don't need any other knowledge except knowing the boiling point of tetraethyl lead. Give up, Quin! You have no chance of tracing the killer with this poison. It's possible he overheard two toxicologists, or even two doctors who had heard of this poison, mention it. The rest is easy. I'm not saying it's definitely so, the killer could be a chemist. But I'm just telling you all the possibilities.\" [1351] \"I suppose the poison was put in the whiskey, right, doctor?\" Quine asked, looking bewildered. [1352] \"That's certain,\" the toxicologist replied. \"There was a large amount of whiskey components in his stomach. Of course, it's easy to poison someone this way. Anyway, most whiskeys bought now have an ether flavor. Moreover, Field may have drunk it down without realizing it was tainted - if he could realize it at all.\" [1353] \"He wouldn't taste it, would he?\" Ellery asked wearily. [1354] \"I've never tasted it, young man, so I'm not sure,\" Dr. Jones replied somewhat sharply. \"But I doubt he could taste it - at least not enough to raise his suspicions. And once he drank it, whether he realized it or not made no difference.\" [1355] Quine turned to Plaut, who was now asleep and his cigar had gone out. \"Hey, doctor!\" [1356] Plaut opened his eyes sleepily. \"Where are my slippers - I can't seem to find them, damn it!\" [1357] Despite the tense atmosphere, the assistant coroner's words made everyone laugh. When he realized what he had said, he smiled and said, \"That's just proof that I should go home and sleep, Quine. What else do you want to know?\" [1358] \"Tell me,\" Quine said, still trembling slightly, \"what did you find out from the whiskey analysis?\" [1359] \"Oh!\" Plaut immediately woke up. \"The whiskey in the bottle is just like the other whiskeys I've tested - nothing wrong with it. These years, my main job has been to test the components of the alcohol. The smell of whiskey on Field's body made me think he had drunk some cheap stuff. But the Scottish whiskey and rye whiskey you brought me from his apartment are both very high quality. They may have been purchased from the same place, in fact. They could be imported. Since the war, I haven't seen such high-quality alcohol in the country - except for those stored before the war... I think Wiley has already given you my report, and the ginger ale is also fine.\" [1360] Quine nodded. \"Well, that seems to be settled,\" he said gravely. \"We seem to have made no progress on tetraethyl lead. But just to be on the safe side, doctor - work with the professors here to see if there are any flaws in the distribution of the poison in the body. You two are very familiar with it, and it will be more effective than other people. It's just a shot in the dark, and it may not yield any results.\" [1361] \"That's fine,\" Ellery whispered. \"Every novelist has to write until the end.\" [1362] * * *\n[1363] \"I think,\" after the two left, Ellery said urgently, \"I should go to the bookstore and buy Faulkner's novel.\" [1364] \"Sit here!\" the policeman shouted, pulling him back to the chair. \"No. That damn book won't run away. I want you to sit here, I have a headache, and you're not going anywhere.\" [1365] Ellery sat on the cushion, sighing. \"Whenever I think about how all the investigations into human weaknesses are useless and a waste of time, my respected father puts such a responsibility on me again. Hey! What do you think?\" [1366] \"I'm not putting any responsibility on you,\" Quine growled, \"Stop exaggerating, I'm already dizzy. I want you to help me straighten out this mess of a case and see - uh, if we can find anything.\" [1367] \"I'm skeptical,\" Ellery said, \"Where do I start?\" [1368] \"You don't need to say anything,\" dad grumbled, \"Tonight, I'll talk, you listen. You might want to take some notes. [1369] \"Let's start with Field. First, I think we can safely assume that our friend didn't go to the theater Monday night for entertainment, but for business. Agreed?\" [1370] \"In my opinion, that's a given,\" Ellery said, \"How did Willy report Field's activities on Monday?\" [1371] \"Field went to the office at 9:30\u2014he usually gets there in the morning. He worked until noon. There were no private visitors all morning. At 12:00, he had lunch alone at the Webster Club, returned to the office at 1:30, and worked until 4:00\u2014he seemed to go straight home after work, as the doorman and elevator operator can confirm he was back in his apartment at 4:30. Michaels went to him at 5:00 and left at 6:00, and Willy found no other information. Field left the apartment at 7:30, dressed as we found him. I have a list of clients he saw that day, but it's not very useful.\" [1372] \"What's the reason for his low bank account balance?\" Ellery asked. [1373] \"As I suspected,\" Quinn answered, \"Field has been losing a lot of money in the stock market\u2014not to mention he also wastes money at the racetrack. For a sensible man, he's a gullible fool. Anyway, that explains why his personal account cash is so low. Moreover, perhaps it can more firmly indicate that the '50,000' we found on the promotional material refers to money. And this money, I'm sure, is somehow related to the person he was supposed to meet at the theater.\" [1374] \"Now, I think we can safely conclude that Field is very familiar with the killer. On the one hand, he accepted the drink offered to him without the slightest suspicion, not even asking; on the other hand, they arranged to meet in a place that seemed to be for concealment\u2014otherwise, why choose the theater?\" [1375] \"Well, I'll ask you the same question,\" Ellery interrupted him, pursing his lips, \"Why choose the theater as the meeting place? It's obvious that they were going to engage in a secret, criminal transaction. Why not the park, which is more concealed? Or the hotel lobby, which is also good? How do you answer that?\" [1376] \"Unfortunately, my son,\" the detective said mildly, \"Field didn't suspect anyone was going to kill him. In his mind, he just had to do his part in the transaction. In fact, it's likely that Field chose the theater as the meeting place himself. Perhaps he wanted to create an alibi for himself. We can't know for sure what he was trying to do. As for the hotel lobby - he would have risked being seen by others. And perhaps he didn't want to go to a secluded place like the park. Finally, he may have had a specific reason not to be seen with the other person. Don't forget, the ticket stubs we found show that the other person didn't go into the theater with Field. But these are all pointless speculations -\"\n[1377] Ellery smiled thoughtfully but said nothing. He thought to himself that the old man hadn't fully answered his question, and for a detective like Quin, who was more direct in his thinking, this was unusual...\n[1378] But Quin continued speaking. \"Well, we must always remember that the person who Field was dealing with may not be the killer. Of course, this is just one possibility. This crime seems to have been well-planned. But if that person isn't the killer, we need to find two people in the audience on Monday night who have a direct connection to Field's death.\" [1379] \"Morgan?\" Ellery asked casually. [1380] The detective shrugged. \"Perhaps. Why didn't he tell us that when we spoke to him yesterday afternoon? He confessed to everything else. Well, maybe he felt that paying a blackmail fee to a murdered man and being at the theater was enough indirect evidence.\" [1381] \"Let's look at this,\" Ellery said. \"We found a number '5,000' written on the flyer by the deceased, which obviously refers to money. Through Sampson and Cronin, we know that Field was a despicable man and possibly a criminal. Moreover, we learned from Morgan that he was good at extortion. Therefore, I think we can safely assume that he went to the Roman Theater on Monday night to collect or arrange the payment of the $50,000 ransom from someone we don't know yet. So far, it all makes sense, doesn't it?\" [1382] \"Continue,\" the inspector grumbled without expressing his opinion. [1383] \"Okay,\" Ellery continued, \"if we assume that the person who was being extorted and the murderer are the same person, we don't need to look for a motive anymore. The motive is clear - to stop Field from continuing to extort. However, if we assume that the murderer and the person being extorted are two different people, we have to continue looking for his motive. My view is that there is no need to do so - the murderer and the person being extorted are the same. What do you think?\" [1384] \"I tend to agree with you, Ellery,\" the inspector said. \"I only mentioned another possibility - without reaching a conclusion. So, let's continue on the assumption that the murderer and the person being extorted are the same...\"\n[1385] \"Now - I want to find out about the missing tickets.\" [1386] \"Ah \u2013 missing tickets,\" Ellery murmured. \"I wonder what you can make of that?\" [1387] \"Be serious now, you little rascal!\" Quin shouted. \"That's all I know. Overall, we have to deal with eight seats \u2013 one of which belongs to Field, and we found the stub on him; one belongs to the killer, and Flint found the stub for us; and finally, there are six empty seats, with tickets sold according to the ticket office records, but nowhere in the theater or the ticket office were the stubs found, either torn or intact \u2013 nothing at all. First, it's highly unlikely that all six tickets were in the theater on Monday evening, and then someone took them out. Remember, the search of everyone that night wasn't very thorough, and it's possible that we overlooked something small like a ticket. However, that's basically impossible. The best explanation is that either Field or the killer bought eight tickets, intending to use two and leave the other six, ensuring that they could not be disturbed during their brief transaction. In this case, the wisest course of action would be to destroy the tickets as soon as they were purchased. It could have been done by either Field or the killer, depending on who arranged the meeting. So, we must forget about those six tickets \u2013 they no longer exist, and we'll never find them. [1388] \"Next,\" the detective continued, \"we learned that Field and the person he was extorting entered the theater separately. This can be inferred from the fact that when I put the two stubs together, the tear didn't line up. If two people entered the theater together, the tickets would be handed to the usher together, and they would be torn together. This doesn't mean they didn't enter the theater at the same time; they may have come in one after the other for safety, pretending not to know each other. However, Maggie O'Connell claimed that no one sat in LL30 during the first act, and Jesse Lynch, the boy selling oranges, confirmed that no one sat there for at least ten minutes after the second act began. That means either the killer hadn't entered the theater yet or he had come in but sat somewhere else \u2013 he bought another ticket. \" [1389] Ellery shook his head. [1390] \"I realized that too, son,\" the old man said, a little irritated. \"I was just working through the thought process. What I meant to say was that it seems highly unlikely that the killer entered the theater at the time of the performance; he probably came in after the second act started and at least ten minutes later.\" [1391] \"I can prove that,\" Ellery said lazily. [1392] The officer took a pinch of snuff. \"I know - the mysterious numbers on the flyers. What do you make of them?\" [1393] 930\n[1394] 815\n[1395] 50,000\n[1396] \"We know what 50,000 represents. The other two numbers, however, do not refer to money but to time. First, '815' \u2013 the play started at 8:25. It is likely that Field arrived around 8:15 or even earlier, as he glanced at his watch for some reason. Now, let's assume that he had an appointment with someone, but that person was late. It is very likely that Field jotted down the numbers on the flyer casually \u2013 first, '50,000', indicating the transaction he was considering, including the $50,000 ransom; then 8:15, the time he was considering the matter; and finally 9:30 \u2013 the time the victim should have arrived! Writing these things down was natural for Field, as anyone with a habit of scribbling in their spare time would do so. We are lucky, as this indicates two things: first, the exact time Field met with the killer was 9:30; second, it corresponds to the time we hypothesize the murder took place. At 9:25, Lynch saw Field alive and alone; by 9:30, according to what Field wrote, the killer should have arrived, and we naturally assume the killer did arrive at the theater; and according to Dr. Jones, the poison that killed Field took 15 to 20 minutes to take effect \u2013 considering that Paskack found the body at 9:55, we can infer that the poison was taken at 9:35. If the tetrachloroethylene took 20 minutes to take effect \u2013 that would be 9:55. Of course, the killer had already left the crime scene by then. Don't forget, he couldn't have known that Mr. Paskack would suddenly stand up and leave his seat. The killer may have thought that Field's body would not be discovered until intermission, at 10:05, so Field would have been dead and unable to leave any further information. The killer was undoubtedly lucky, as he discovered Field too late and did not leave any other information, only stating that he was murdered. If Paskack had left five minutes earlier, we would have caught the cunning killer by now.\" [1397] \"Great!\" Ellery murmured, his smile full of affection, \"That was well stated, congratulations!\" [1398] \"Oh, go to hell,\" his father barked, \"I was just repeating what you said Monday night in Penczel's office \u2013 in fact, although the killer left the crime scene between 9:30 and 9:55, he was still in the theater for the rest of the evening, until we got everyone to leave.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 8:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:52 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nHe was sent to Elmira on a 'minor theft' charge thanks to Field's clever manipulation of the law. However, Field possessed evidence of the true crime Mickles had committed and hid it in his favorite collection, intending to use it at a later date. This Field, what a cunning man... After Mickles was released, Field used these documents to coerce Mickles into doing various tasks for him without any remorse. [2192] \"For a long time, Michael has been searching for those files. His eagerness, you can imagine. He would go to the apartment whenever he had the chance. After one failure after another, he became desperate. I have no doubt that when Field found out that Michael was rummaging through his apartment day after day, he must have been laughing behind his back... On Monday night, Michael went home to sleep as he said. But on Tuesday morning, when he read the newspaper and learned that Field had been murdered, he realized it was all over. He had to search desperately for those files - if he couldn't find them, the police might find them, and then he would be finished. So, on Tuesday morning, he risked being caught by the police and returned to Field's house. Of course, the whole story about the check was just nonsense. [2193] \"But let's go back to Barry. We found a file labeled 'Miscellaneous' in the hat, detailing an unsavory matter. To abbreviate, Stephen Barry has black blood in his veins. Born into a poor family in the South, there is concrete evidence - letters, birth certificates, and so on - proving his black heritage. As we know, Field has made a business out of pursuing such things. He somehow acquired these documents, how long ago we do not know, but it must have been a long time. He had then discovered Barry's identity, finding him a struggling actor living in poverty and penniless. Field decided to leave him be for the time being. Once Barry made it big or became famous, there would be plenty of time to extort him. What Field did not anticipate was Barry's engagement to the daughter of a millionaire, the aristocratic socialite Frances Evans-Pope. It goes without saying that if Frances Evans-Pope were to find out about Barry's mixed heritage, the consequences would be dire. Furthermore, it is crucial to note that Barry had become impoverished due to gambling. His earnings had gone into the pockets of the racecourse bookmaker's agent, and he owed a significant amount of money, which he would never be able to repay with his current means, unless he married Frances. In fact, he was so destitute that he secretly urged her to marry him as soon as possible. I have always been curious about how sincerely he treated Frances. However, to be fair, I do not believe he married her solely for the money. I think he genuinely loved her - but who doesn't? But who doesn't?\" [2194] The old man smiled as he reminisced, continuing, \"Not long ago, Field brought those documents to Barry \u2013 of course, secretly. Barry gave him all the money he could, but it was barely enough to satisfy the insatiable extortionist. With no other option, he could only keep delaying Field. But Field himself was in a bind due to gambling, trying to 'recoup' funds through one small deal after another. Barry was at a dead end, realizing that unless he made Field forever silent, he would lose everything. So he planned this murder. He understood that even if he somehow managed to gather the $50,000 Field demanded \u2013 clearly an impossible task \u2013 even if he got the original documents, Field would still ruin his hopes by spreading the word. The only way out was \u2013 eliminate Field. And he did.\" [2195] \"Black blood, huh?\" Crooning grumbled, \"Poor fellow.\" [2196] \"You couldn't tell from his looks,\" said Sampson, \"he looked just as white as you and me.\" [2197] \"Barry is not like a pure black man,\" the officer countered, \"He has one drop of black blood in his veins \u2013 just one drop, but that's enough for the Evans-Pope family... Continue on. After reading these documents, we understood everything: who did it, how it was done, and the motive. But we have to make a judgment on the evidence to convict. You can't accuse someone of murder without evidence... er, what do we have? Nothing! [2198] \"Let's discuss some useful clues that could serve as evidence. The handbag of the woman - eliminate. It's worthless, you know. The source of the poison - can't find it. By the way, Barry did it according to Dr. Jones' method - the toxicologist Jones. Barry bought regular gasoline, distilled it to get tetraethyl lead. No trace left. Another possible clue - Monte Field's hat, but it's gone... Six empty tickets - we've never seen them, and the chances of seeing them are slim. The only physical evidence - those documents - only explain the motive, but can't prove anything. With this evidence, any member of Morgan or Field's gang could have committed the crime.\" [2199] \"The only hope of securing incriminating evidence lies in sending someone to sneak into Barry's apartment at night, hoping to find the hat, tickets, or other clues, such as poison or poisonous instruments. I have engaged a specialist in burglary for this purpose, who will break into Barry's apartment on Friday night while he is at the theater performing, but no such clues have been found. The hat, tickets, and poison have all been destroyed. It is clear that Barry did it, we can be sure.\" [2200] \"I gathered a group of Monday night viewers in desperation and held a meeting in the hope that someone would remember seeing Barry that night. You know, sometimes people forget things due to tension or excitement, but then they remember later. But this time it didn't work either. The only valuable testimony was from the boy selling orange juice, who saw Field pick up a handbag used at the evening party in the alley. However, this had nothing to do with Barry. Remember, on Thursday night, when we questioned the cast members, we didn't get any direct evidence either. [2201] \"So we have a perfect, hypothetical statement of fact to present to the jury, with no concrete evidence. A clever defense lawyer could easily refute our case. It's mostly indirect evidence based on reasoning. You and I both know how much of a chance we have in court... Then, my real trouble came, and Ellery was leaving town. [2202] \"I racked my brain - I was left with little. How can I convict someone without evidence? It's so frustrating. Ellery sent me a telegram with a suggestion that helped me one last time.\" [2203] \"A suggestion?\" asked Cronin. [2204] \"He suggested that I personally engage in extortion...\"\n[2205] \"You personally extort?\" Sampson looked at him. \"I don't understand.\" [2206] \"Trust Ellery, he's good at solving puzzles.\" The police officer refutes, \"I immediately understand that the only thing I can do is fabricate evidence!\" [2207] Sampson and Cronin frown in confusion. [2208] \"It's simple,\" Quine says, \"Field was killed by an unusual poison. The reason Field was killed was because he blackmailed Barry. If Barry was blackmailed in the same situation, he would use poison again - probably the same poison. My hypothesis is reasonable, right? I don't need to say that you both know, 'once bitten, twice shy.' As for Barry, if I can lure him into using tetraethyl lead on someone else, I can catch him! This poison is almost unknown - but I don't need to explain further. You can see that if I catch him with tetraethyl lead on him, that's all the evidence I need. [2209] \"How to accomplish this feat is another matter... The situation was perfect for blackmailing. In fact, I have the original documents about Barry's background and impure blood. Barry thought these documents had been destroyed - he had no reason to suspect that the ones he got from Field were carefully forged. If I blackmail him, then his situation will be the same again. So he must resort to the same tactics. [2210] \"So I took advantage of our friend Charles Michaels. I used Michaels only because, in Barry's eyes, Michaels was Field's friend, henchman, and loyal partner, and it was reasonable for him to possess the original documents. I had Michaels write a letter, dictated by me. I had Michaels write it because Barry had dealt with Field and might recognize Michaels' handwriting. This seemed like a small problem, but I couldn't take any risks. If this plan went wrong, Barry would immediately recognize it, and I would never catch him.\" [2211] \"In the letter, I attached an original document to show that this new blackmail threat was potent. I claimed that Field had given Barry a copy - the attached document proved my point. Barry didn't doubt that Michaels was imitating his master and blackmailing him. The wording was like a final ultimatum. I set a time and place, and in short, the scheme worked...\n[2212] \"I think that's all, gentlemen. Barry came, with his faithful little syringe subcutaneously injected with tetraethyl lead, and a bottle of wine - you see, except for the location, it was exactly the same as the Field case. I instructed my man - Ritter - not to take a risk. As soon as he recognized Barry, he aimed his gun at him and sounded the alarm. Fortunately, we were almost right behind them in the bushes. Barry was desperate, and if he had had even the slightest chance, he would have killed Ritter and committed suicide.\" [2213] The officer finished speaking and sighed, leaning forward to snort some snuff, and the room fell silent. [2214] Sampson shifted in his chair. \"It sounds like a thriller, Quin,\" he said admiringly, \"but there are a few things I don't understand. For example, since tetraethyl lead is unknown to people, how did Barry discover it - to the point where he could refine it himself?\" [2215] \"Oh,\" the officer smiled, \"Ever since Jones described this poison, this has bothered me. Even after catching Barry, I was still in the dark. But this shows how stupid I am - the answer has been right in front of my nose. Do you remember, when we were at the Eves-Pope estate, a doctor named Cornish was introduced? This doctor is a private friend of the old financier, and both of them are interested in medicine. In fact, I remember Ellery asking, 'Did Eves-Pope recently donate $100,000 to the Chemical Research Foundation?' Indeed, it was. One evening a few months ago, a meeting was held at the Eves-Pope house, and Barry happened to hear about tetraethyl lead. Through Cornish's introduction, a group of scientists visited this financial giant to request funding for the foundation. The evening's conversation naturally turned to rumors in medicine and recent scientific discoveries. Barry admitted that he had overheard the council member, a famous toxicologist, introducing this poison to the members. At the time, Barry didn't think he would ever use this knowledge; when he decided to kill Field, he saw the superiority of this poison and its difficult traceability.\" [2216] \"What was the significance of the message you sent me through Louis Ponzel on Thursday night, Inspector?\" Cromwell asked curiously, \"Do you remember? You asked me to observe whether Ponzel and Ponzel knew each other when they met at Louin? As I reported to you, I asked Louin, and he denied knowing Ponzel. What's going on?\" [2217] \"Ponzel,\" the policeman whispered, \"Ponzel always piques my curiosity, Tim. When I sent him to you, remember, I hadn't made any deductions about the hat... I sent him to you out of curiosity. I thought that if Louin recognized him, it might suggest a connection between Ponzel and Field. My idea wasn't confirmed, and I didn't have high hopes to begin with. Ponzel might have known Field outside, while Louin didn't know. On the other hand, I didn't want Ponzel to hang around the theater that morning; it was beneficial for both of us for him to run errands.\" [2218] \"Oh, I hope you're satisfied with the package of newspapers I sent you according to your request,\" Cromwell smiled. [2219] \"What about the anonymous letter that Morgan received? Was it a cover-up or something else?\" Sampson asked. [2220] \"It was a deceptive trick,\" Quayne said solemnly. \"Barry explained it to me last night. He heard that Morgan threatened to kill Field. Of course, he didn't know that Field was blackmailing Morgan. But he thought that if he could trick Morgan into coming to the theater on Monday night, it would create a strong false lead. If Morgan didn't come, there would be no loss. That's how he operated: he chose ordinary cheap paper, put on gloves, and printed the letter at a print shop in a messy way, signed it with a meaningless initial, and then mailed it from the post office. He was careful with his fingerprints, so he couldn't be traced from the letter. Coincidentally, Morgan fell for it and came\n[2221] Sampson nodded. \"I have one more question. How did Barry arrange for the tickets? Was it all his doing?\" [2222] \"Of course, it was. Barry convinced Field that, for fairness, the meeting and the document exchange should take place secretly in the theater. Field agreed and was easily convinced to buy eight tickets at the ticket office. He realized that, for secrecy, he needed to buy six additional tickets. He gave Barry seven and kept one for himself, which he destroyed immediately.\" [2223] The officer stood up, smiling tiredly. \"Juna! Add some coffee,\" he whispered. [2224] Sampson waved Juna away. \"Thank you, Quin, I have to go. Cromwell and I have a lot of things to deal with regarding this gang. But I can't leave until I hear the whole story from you... Quin, old friend, I truly mean it, you did an incredible job.\" [2225] \"I've never heard of such a thing,\" Cromwell said sincerely. \"A puzzle to solve, and such a perfect reasoning from start to finish!\" [2226] \"Really?\" the officer asked calmly. \"I'm glad, gentlemen. All the credit goes to Ellery. I'm so proud of my son...\"\n[2227] Sampson and Cronin left, and Juna went back to the kitchen to wash the breakfast dishes. The officer turned to the desk, picked up a pen, and quickly read the letter he had written to his son. He sighed and continued writing. [2228] Forget what I just wrote. It's been over an hour since then. Sampson and Cronin came by, and I told them all about the work we did on the case. I've never seen such a pair! They were like children, listening to a fairy tale... As I said, I know how little I've done, and how much you've done. I'm looking forward to you finding a good girl to marry, and then the Quin family will settle down and live peacefully in Italy...\n[2229] When are you coming home? Don't think I'm rushing you, but I'm so lonely, son. I... no, I mean, I'm selfish and tired. An old relic like me needs some care. You'll be home soon, won't you? Juna sends his greetings. That mischievous fellow is washing dishes in the kitchen, making my ears ring. [2230] Love, Dad\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 9:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:52 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nFinally, he turned around and said, \"Well, gentlemen, that's it for tonight. Let's go.\" [762] On the sidewalk, the police had cordoned off a large area with ropes, and a few curious people stood around, yawning. [763] \"Even at two in the morning, these night owls are still prowling on Broadway,\" Sampson grumbled. He offered to give Quinn and his son a ride home, but they politely declined, so he waved goodbye and got in the car. A group of hardworking reporters rushed over the rope and surrounded Quinn and his son. [764] \"What are you doing, gentlemen?\" the old man asked, frowning. [765] \"How did the progress of tonight's work go, officer?\" one reporter asked eagerly. [766] \"Boys, you can get the information you want from Chief Weli \u2013 he's still inside.\" The reporters rushed into the glass door, and Qian chuckled. [767] Ellery and Richard stood silently by the roadside, watching as the police chased the reporters out. Inspector Qian suddenly said, \"Come on, son, let's walk home for a while.\" His tone conveyed exhaustion. [768] This book is compiled by \"ePUBw.COM\", which provides the latest and most complete high-quality e-book downloads! [769] Part Two\n[770] ... For example, one time, young Gene C came to me after a month of diligently investigating a difficult case. He looked desperate and silently handed me a piece of paper. After reading the contents, I was surprised to find that it was his resignation letter. [771] \"Hey, Gene!\" I called out, \"What's this all about?\" [772] \"I failed, Mr. Brierly,\" he whispered, \"A month's work gone to waste. My investigative approach was wrong, it's embarrassing.\" [773] \"Gene, my friend,\" I said solemnly, \"You're quitting over this?\" I tear his resignation letter into pieces, and he looks at me in shock. \"Go ahead,\" I advise, \"Start over. Remember the adage: One must know what is wrong before he knows what is right.\" [774] - Excerpt from Augustin Brierly's memoirs, \"Recollections of a Chief of Police\"\n[775] Chapter Eight: Quinn Father and Son Meet Field's Best Friend\n[776] The Quinn's apartment is located on West 87th Street. From the pipe rack above the fireplace to the gleaming military swords on the wall, it is easy to see that this is a typical man's residence. The house is made of brown bricks and has been preserved from the Victorian era. There are three households living in it, and the Quinn family resides on the top floor. As you climb the stairs covered in thick carpeting, you walk through a seemingly endless, dimly lit rectangular corridor, and just when you think that only a mummy would live in such a gloomy place, you arrive at a massive oak door with the name \"Quinns\" written on it in neat handwriting, framed in a border. Then, Junna peeks out from behind the door, grinning at you, and you enter a whole new world. [777] Not only one person has left their cozy nest to willingly climb these unpleasant stairs to seek refuge here. Not only one famous name has been unceremoniously taken from the doorway to the living room by Junna. [778] In fact, the entrance hall was inspired by Ellery. It is small and narrow, so the surrounding walls appear very high and unnatural. One wall is completely covered by a tapestry depicting a hunting scene - a tapestry that is the most suitable decoration for this medieval-style room, conveying a sense of humor and solemnity. The Quinn family deeply dislikes this tapestry but still keeps it because it was a gift from a impulsive duke, expressing gratitude from the royal family, as Richard Quinn had helped the duke's son avoid a scandal, although the details have never been made public. Beneath the tapestry is a heavy pulpit table with a lambskin lamp and a pair of bronze bookends holding a three-volume set of \"One Thousand and One Nights.\" [779] Additionally, there are two pulpit chairs and a small carpet in the entrance hall. [780] It is always dimly lit and unbearable here. However, when you pass through this oppressive place, you will see a pleasantly wide room that you would never expect. This stark contrast is also the humor of Ellery, and without him, the old man would have thrown the entrance hall and its furniture into some dark corner. [781] The living room has bookshelves on three walls, emitting the smell of leather, stacked up to the ceiling. On the other wall, there is a huge primitive fireplace with a hard oak mantelpiece, with glowing ironwork as the grate dividing the fireplace. Above the fireplace, there is a famous crossbow knife, a gift from a swordsmanship coach in Nuremberg to Richard, as the young Richard lived with him during his studies in Germany. The room is brightly lit, illuminating the entire cluttered and spacious area, with armchairs, sofa, footstools, and brightly colored leather cushions everywhere. In other words, for two high-intelligence gentlemen with refined taste, this is the most comfortable room they could design. However, the room is cluttered and messy, and after a while, the air becomes foul. Juno, however, can prevent this from happening. He is a jack-of-all-trades, serving as a janitor, housekeeper, butler, personal servant, and the family's lucky charm, keeping the entire room in order. [782] When Ellery was at university, the elder Quinn felt lonely, and Juno was adopted by Quinn during that time. He is a cheerful young man, nineteen years old, and has been an orphan since he can remember. He is always happy and has never realized that he needs a surname. He is slender and short, a bit timid, but very cheerful. Sometimes he is so excited that he dances around, and at other times, he is quiet as a mouse. This Juno worships the elder Quinn like an ancient Alaskan idolizing their totem. He has a very close relationship with Ellery, although both of them are not very talkative, but they can show it through the boy's attentive service. Juno lives in a small room next to the Quinn father and son's bedroom, and Richard once joked, \"You can hear a flea singing to its mate in the middle of the night.\" [783] The night of the murder of Monte Field has passed. The next morning, just as Juno was laying the tablecloth for breakfast, the phone rang. The boy was used to this situation and picked up the phone. [784] \"I am Juno, the servant of Detective Quinn. Who is this, please?\" [785] \"Oh, it's you,\" a deep voice growled over the phone. \"You little bastard, wake up the detective, quickly!\" [786] \"Unless Junna knows who is calling, Inspector Quin cannot be disturbed,\" Junna knew well the voice of Wiley, smiled and stuck out his tongue. [787] A skinny hand tightly grasped Junna's neck, pushing him to the center of the room. Inspector Quin was dressed and ready, still in a daze from snorting snuff, his nostrils still quivering. He spoke into the phone, \"Ignore Junna, Thomas. What's going on? This is Quin.\" [788] \"Oh, it's you, Inspector? I didn't want to call you so early, but Ritter just called from Field's apartment. His report is interesting,\" Wiley said in a low voice. [789] \"Good, good!\" Quin chuckled. \"So, our friend Ritter caught someone, right, Thomas? Who is it?\" [790] \"Guess, Inspector,\" a voice came over the phone, indifferent. \"He said he caught a lady, and the situation was awkward. Ritter said he would divorce his wife if he spent any more time with her. Any orders, Inspector?\" [791] Quinn bursts into laughter. \"Of course, Thomas. Send a few people over to accompany him right away. I'll be there soon too\u2014that is, I'll wake Ellery up and come over.\" [792] He hangs up the phone and grins. \"Juno! The boy hears the call and pops his head out from behind the kitchen door. \"Hurry up and prepare eggs and coffee, boy!\" He turns and walks towards the bedroom, finding Ellery, although not wearing a tie, getting dressed and looking thoughtful. [793] \"Ha, you're up?\" Quinn mutters, sitting down in a chair. \"I thought I'd have to drag you out of bed, you lazybones!\" [794] \"Looks like you slept well.\" Ellery says nonchalantly. \"I'm up, and I don't want to sleep anymore. As soon as Juno prepares breakfast, I'll eat and go out, and I won't bother you.\" He lazily walks back to the bedroom and comes out again, straightening his collar and tie. [795] \"Hey, where are you going, kid?\" Quinn shouts, suddenly standing up. [796] \"To the bookstore, dear detective,\" Ellery says logically, \"You don't think I'd miss the first edition of Faulkner's novel, do you? It might still be there, you know.\" [797] \"More of Faulkner's boring stuff,\" Quinn says sternly, \"Help yourself. Hey - Juno - where's that boy?\" [798] Juno bustles into the room, carrying a tray and a jug of milk. In a flash, he sets the table with steaming coffee and toasted bread. Father and son eat their breakfast in silence. [799] \"Now,\" Ellery puts down his cup, \"since I've finished this rustic breakfast, you can tell me where the fire is.\" [800] \"Put on your hat and coat, stop asking pointless questions, kid,\" Quinn barks. Within three minutes, they're out on the sidewalk, hailing a taxi. [801] The taxi stopped in front of a large apartment building. Detective Piggott was strolling down the sidewalk, smoking a cigarette. Quin gave him a nod, and they hurried into the building. They were rushed to the fourth floor, where Detective Haggstrom greeted them and pointed to the apartment door with the number 4-D. Ellery leaned forward, reading the nameplate, about to make a witty remark, when the door swung open with a bang, revealing Detective Right's flushed, round face. [802] \"Good morning, officers,\" Detective Right mumbled as he opened the door, \"I'm so glad you're here, sir.\" [803] Quin and Ellery strode into the room. They stood in a lavishly decorated foyer, facing a living room with a closed door, from which they could see a frilly lady's slipper and a delicate ankle. [804] Detective Quin took a few steps forward, then changed his mind and quickly opened the door, calling out to Detective Haggstrom who was still standing outside. [805] \"Come in,\" Detective Quin barked, \"I have a job for you.\" [806] Sitting in the room was a mature and attractive woman. She suddenly stood up, nervously stamping out her cigarette with her foot. She looked tired, with thick makeup covering her pale complexion; she wore a flowing negligee and her hair was disheveled. [807] \"Are you the one in charge here?\" she snapped at Quinn, her anger rising. Quinn stood motionless, his face cold and expressionless. \"What do you mean, sending a cop over here, locking me in this room all night? Ah?\" [808] She lunged at Quinn, as if to grapple with the old detective. Ritter quickly stepped forward, grabbed her arm. \"Hey, you,\" he barked, \"shut up and speak when spoken to.\" [809] She glared at Ritter, then suddenly twisted out of his grip and sat down in a chair, glaring at them. [810] \"What's going on here?\" she demanded, her eyes blazing with anger. Quinn stood there, unmoving, his face still cold and expressionless. \"What do you mean, sending a cop over here, locking me in this room all night, huh?\" [811] Quinn stood with his hands on his hips, looking her up and down with disgust. Ellery glanced at the woman quickly, then began to wander around the room, gazing at the wall ornaments and a Japanese painting, before picking up a book from the coffee table and retreating to another dark corner. [812] Quinn gestured to Hargstrom. \"Take this lady to the next room and keep her company for a while.\" The officer unceremoniously pushed the woman to her feet. She shook her head defiantly and strode into the adjacent room, with Hargstrom following. [813] \"Now, Ritter, my boy,\" the old man sighed, sitting down in an armchair. \"Tell me what happened.\" [814] Ritter answered the old man's questions, his eyes tired and bloodshot. \"I did exactly as you ordered. I came in a police car. I wasn't sure if anyone was watching, so I parked around the corner and walked to the apartment door. It was quiet and there were no lights on \u2013 I looked into the windows at the back of the building before coming in. So I rang the doorbell and waited for a response. [815] \"Nobody answered,\" Ritter continued, tightening his jaw. \"I rang the bell again \u2013 longer and louder this time. This time there was a response. I heard the sound of the door latch being unlocked, and then a woman's voice cooed, 'Is that you, dear? Where's your key?' Ha \u2013 I thought \u2013 it was Field's girlfriend! So I stepped inside and, before she could even react, I grabbed her. Ah, sir, I was surprised. I had expected \u2013 well, her attire, but I grabbed a thin silk nightgown. I'm sure my face must have turned red \u2013\" He said, embarrassed. \"Well, I'll be!\" [816] \"Ha! That's a rare opportunity for us upholders of the law,\" Ellery murmured, looking down at a painted vase. [817] \"Anyway,\" Ritter continued, \"after I grabbed her, she started screaming \u2013 loudly. I pushed her into the living room, she turned on the lights, and I looked at her closely. She was pale with fear, but still bold enough to start cursing at me, asking who I was, what I was doing in her apartment at night, and so on. I showed her my badge. Detective, the strong, beautiful woman immediately clammed up when she saw my badge, refusing to answer my questions.\" [818] \"Why is that?\" the old man's gaze shifted from the floor to the ceiling, surveying the room's furnishings. [819] \"It's hard to say, detective,\" Ritter said. \"At first, she seemed scared, but after seeing the badge, she became more confident. The longer I stayed here, the more brazen she became.\" [820] \"You didn't tell her about Field, did you?\" Detective Quinn asked in a low, urgent tone. [821] Ritter glared at his superior. \"Not a word, sir,\" he said. \"I couldn't get anything out of her \u2013 she kept yelling, 'Wait for Monte to come back, you jerk!' \u2013 so I checked the bedroom, which was empty, and then I pushed her into the bedroom, turned on the lights, didn't close the door, and stayed here all night. She eventually climbed into bed, and I think she fell asleep. At around seven this morning, she suddenly came out and started yelling at me again, as if she thought Field had been arrested by headquarters and demanded a newspaper. I told her to calm down and then called the office. That's all that happened since then.\" [822] \"Say, Dad!\" Ellery suddenly called from a corner of the room. \"What do you think our lawyer friend was reading? You'll never guess \u2013 it was 'How to Judge Character from Handwriting!'\" [823] Detective Quin stood up and grumbled, \"Stop messing around with those books and come here.\" [824] He opened the bedroom door, and the woman was sitting on the bed with her legs crossed. The bed was of poor quality and was in the French style, with a thick silk canopy that hung from the ceiling to the floor. Haggstrom was now staring blankly out the window. [825] Detective Quin quickly looked around. He turned to Ritter and whispered, \"Was the bed this messy when you came in last night? \u2013 did it look like someone had slept on it?\" [826] Quinn turned to the woman and walked over to the bedside, sitting next to her and looking at her half-turned face. She lit a cigarette with a disdainful look. [827] \"I'm Inspector Quinn, dear,\" the old man said gently. \"I remind you that if you persist in not answering or lying, you will encounter more trouble. I'm sure you understand that.\" [828] She twisted away. \"I won't answer any of your questions, Inspector, until I know what power you have over me,\" she said. \"I haven't done anything illegal, and I've been behaving properly. Think about it.\" [829] Quinn took a pinch of snuff, as if the woman's words reminded him of his favorite thing. He spoke in a pleasant voice, \"That's fair, you see, all alone, rushing out of bed in the middle of the night - were you in bed then?\" [830] Quinn took a pinch of snuff, as if the woman's words reminded him of his favorite thing. He spoke in a pleasant voice, \"That's fair, you see, all alone, rushing out of bed in the middle of the night - were you in bed then?\" [831] \"Of course I was in bed,\" she said immediately, biting her lips tightly. [832] \"\u2014facing a police officer... I don't think you were scared, my dear.\" [833] \"No!\" she exclaimed. [834] \"Well, we won't argue about that,\" the old man said kindly, \"but you don't mind telling me your name, do you?\" [835] \"I don't know why I should tell you, but it won't do me any harm.\" The woman argued, \"My name is Angela Lasso\u2014Mrs. Angela Lasso\u2014well, I'm engaged to Field.\" [836] \"I see, Mrs. Angela Roxo,\" Quine said gravely. \"You're engaged to Field. Well, what were you doing in the room last night, Mrs. Angela Roxo?\" [837] \"It's none of your business!\" she said coldly. \"You'd better let me go now\u2014I haven't done anything illegal. You have no right to ask me these ridiculous questions, old man!\" [838] Ellery stood in a corner, looking out the window, and smiled when he heard this. Quine leaned over to the woman and gently took her hand. [839] \"My dear Mrs. Roxo,\" he said, \"believe me\u2014we're so eager to know what you were doing here last night that we have good reason. Now, tell me.\" [840] \"I won't say anything until I know how you treated Field!\" she cried, shaking off Quine's hand. \"If you've caught him, why are you bothering me? I know nothing!\"\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 10:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:52 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nWait for my orders there. If you can get in, get in; otherwise, stand guard outside all night.\" [166] \"Yes, sir,\" Hess also left. [167] Quinn turned around and chuckled as he saw Ellery bending over the dead body with his broad shoulders. [168] \"Don't trust your father, huh, Ellery?\" the officer scolded. \"What are you snooping around for?\" [169] Ellery straightened up and smiled. \"I was just a little curious,\" he said. \"This unpleasant corpse has some things that interest me. For example, have you measured this man's head?\" He pulled a rope out of his coat pocket and handed it to his father to examine. [170] The officer took the rope, looking stern, and called over a policeman standing behind the theater. He whispered some instructions, and the policeman left with the rope. [171] \"Officer.\" [172] Quinn looked up. Hagstrom stood beside him, his eyes shining. [173] \"I found this pushed aside thing under Feld's seat when I picked up the papers. It was against the back wall.\" [174] He held up a dark green bottle, the kind used by ginger ale manufacturers. The flashy label read \"Perry's Premium Dry Ginger Ale.\" The bottle was half-empty. [175] \"Oh, Hagstrom, stop being coy. Spill it out!\" the officer said bluntly. [176] \"Yes, sir! When I found this bottle under the dead man's seat, I knew he must have had some of this tonight. The cleaning ladies only come every twenty-four hours, and if nobody had drunk it or put it there, it wouldn't have been there. I thought, 'Maybe that's a clue,' so I went to the boy who sells drinks in the theater and had him sell me a bottle of ginger ale. He said \u2013\" Haggstrom chuckled, \"He said the theater doesn't sell ginger ale!\" [177] \"Good thinking, Haggstrom,\" the officer said approvingly. \"Bring that boy here.\" [178] As Haggstrom left, a short, stout man rushed over, his evening suit slightly disheveled. A policeman held his arm firmly. The officer sighed. [179] \"Are you in charge of this, sir?\" the little man growled, straightening his five-foot-two-inch frame and sweating profusely. [180] \"Yes,\" Quinn said sternly. [181] \"So you need to understand,\" the man shouted, \"\u2014let go of my arm, do you hear?\u2014you need to understand, sir\u2026\"\n[182] \"Let go of this gentleman's arm, officer,\" Quine said, his frown deepening. [183] \"\u2026I believe the whole thing is a gross violation of the law! Since the performance was interrupted, my wife, daughter, and I have been sitting here for almost an hour, and your officer won't even let us stand up. This is just too damn disrespectful! Do you think you can make all the audience wait while you have fun over there? I've been watching you\u2014don't think I haven't. You've been dawdling, and we've been sitting here suffering. You need to understand, sir\u2014you need to understand! If you don't let us leave right now, I'm going to call my good friend, the district attorney, Sampson, and I'm going to have you charged!\" [184] Quine, the officer, glared at the short, plump man's ashen face. He sighed and spoke sternly, \"My dear sir, have you ever considered that while you're standing here complaining about being detained for almost an hour, the killer might be among these audience members\u2014maybe even sitting next to your wife and daughter? He's just as eager to leave as you are. If you want to file a complaint with your good friend, the district attorney, you can do so after you leave this theater. For now, I must ask you to return to your seat and wait patiently until we allow you to leave. I hope I've made myself clear.\" [185] Onlookers nearby chuckled, seemingly enjoying the short man's embarrassment. He stormed off, the police officer following silently behind. The officer muttered, \"Idiot!\" and then turned to Wiley. [186] \"You and Poe go to the ticket booth and see if you can find complete tickets for these numbers,\" he said, leaning over the last row and hastily scribbling a set of numbers on the back of an old envelope: LL30L, LL28L, LL26L, KK32L, KK30L, KK28L, and KK26L. He handed the note to Ellery, who left. [187] Ellery had been idly leaning against the back wall of the last row, watching his father and the audience, occasionally studying the layout of the theater. He leaned in close to the officer's ear and said, \"I was thinking about something a bit odd: a popular dud like 'The Gunfight' has seven empty seats around the deceased's seat during the performance.\" [188] \"When did you start suspecting, son?\" Quin said, as Ellery tapped his cane on the floor. Quin shouted, \"Pigott!\" [189] The detective approached. [190] \"Find the usher for this aisle and the doorman outside\u2014the middle-aged man in the aisle\u2014and bring them over.\" [191] After Pigsy leaves, a tousle-haired young man appears next to Quin, wiping his face with a handkerchief. [192] \"How is it, Flint?\" Quin immediately asks. [193] \"I searched the floor like a janitor, officer. If you're looking for a hat in this theater, it must be hidden really well.\" [194] \"All right, Flint, stand by.\" [195] The detective walks away, tired. Ellery says slowly, \"Do you really think your young Diogenes, the Greek philosopher and representative of the Cynic school, can find a high-top hat, Dad?\" [196] The officer grunted. He walked down the aisle to each person, whispering to them. As he walked back to Ellery, his face was expressionless, and the police officer who had gone to measure the rope saluted him. [197] \"What size, officer?\" the officer asked. [198] \"The hat store employee said it was exactly seven and eight and a quarter inches.\" [199] Wiley strode forward, followed closely by Pencier, who looked worried. Ellery leaned forward, eager to hear what Wiley had to say. Quinn's face showed impatience. [200] \"Oh, Thomas,\" he said, \"what did you find at the ticket booth?\" [201] \"Just this, officer,\" Wiley said without expression. \"The seven tickets you gave me weren't on the rack. They were sold at the ticket window, and Pencier couldn't tell me when they were sold. [202] \"You know, Wiley, those tickets could have been transferred to an agent,\" Ellery said. [203] \"I checked, Mr. Quinneke,\" Wiley answered. \"The tickets weren't assigned to any agency. There's a clear record of that.\" [204] Quinneke stood quietly, his gray eyes shining. Then he said, \"In other words, gentlemen, it seems that the play has been sold out since opening night, and seven tickets were bought \u2013 by someone who deliberately forgot to come to the performance.\" [205] Chapter 3: The Unfortunate \"Priest\"\n[206] The case was beginning to take shape, and the four men stood in silence, looking at each other. Ponzer's feet moved nervously, and he coughed tensely; Wiley's face showed intense concentration; Ellery stepped back and gazed at his father's blue-gray tie, lost in thought. [207] Old Quin stood there, stroking his beard. Suddenly, he turned to Wiley. [208] \"Thomas, I have a task for you,\" he said. \"I want you to gather about six uniformed men to check everyone here. They have to record the name and address of each audience member. It's a lot of work and will take a lot of time, but it's probably necessary. And, Thomas, did you question the usher in charge of the upper seats?\" [209] \"I asked that young man some questions,\" Wiley said. \"He was standing at the bottom of the stairs leading to the upper seats, guiding the audience members with tickets for the upper seats. The young man's name is Miller.\" [210] \"That young man was very conscientious,\" Ponzer interjected, rubbing his hands. [211] \"Miller swears that after the second act, no one went up to the upper seats from the orchestra stalls, and no one came down from the upper seats,\" Weill says. [212] \"Well, that slightly reduces your workload,\" Old Quincannon says, listening intently to them. \"Thomas, have your people investigate the boxes and orchestra stalls. Remember, I want the name and address of everyone here, everyone. And, Thomas \u2014\"\n[213] \"What else, officer?\" Weill turns around and asks. [214] \"When you check, ask the audience members to show their ticket stubs for the seats they're sitting in. If someone's ticket stub is missing, mark it next to the person who lost it. If someone has a ticket stub that doesn't match their seat number \u2014 which, of course, is almost impossible, but if it happens, mark it. Can you handle this task, old chap?\" [215] \"Of course!\" Weill mutters, striding away. [216] Quinn Detective took a pinch of snuff and stroked his gray beard. [217] \"Ellery,\" he said, \"I know something's bothering you, out with it, son.\" [218] \"Oh?\" Ellery took off his glasses and blinked, starting to speak slowly. \"My dear father, I was just thinking, how is it that for a man who loves books, there's no such thing as a peaceful haven in this world?\" He sat on the armrest of the chair belonging to the deceased, with a hint of melancholy in his eyes. Suddenly, he laughed and said, \"Remember, there was once a butcher who searched for his most prized knife with forty apprentices, but it was in his mouth all along. Pay attention, don't make the same mistake he did.\" [219] \"Son, you've been quite the traveler lately,\" Detective Quinn said grumpily. \"Flint!\" [220] Flint approached. [221] \"You've already had an interesting task tonight, and there's another one for you. Can you bend your back a little more? I seem to remember you participating in a weightlifting competition at the police sports meet when you were a beat cop.\" [222] \"Yes, sir,\" Flint grinned. \"I think I can handle it.\" [223] \"Good,\" Officer Quin said, his hands in his pockets. \"Your task is