cared, and his eyes darted nervously from Doyle's square face to the floor. Doyle patted his shoulder encouragingly and said to Inspector Quinns, \"Sir, he's a bit frightened, but he's a good kid. I've known him since he was a child. He grew up in my beat. Jesse, answer the officer's question...\"\n[230] \"Well, I... I don't know, sir.\" The boy shuffled his feet and stammered, \"During intermission, we can only sell orange water. We signed a contract \u2013\" He mentioned a well-known beverage production company, \"If we only sell their products, we'll get a good discount. So \u2013\"\n[231] \"I see,\" Inspector Quinn said, \"Is it only during intermission that you can sell drinks?\" [232] \"Yes, sir,\" the boy answered, a little more natural, \"After the performance, the doors to the side alleys will open, and then... my partner and I can come in. Our booths are already set up, and the cups are filled with drinks, ready to serve.\" [233] \"So, there are two of you selling, right?\" [234] \"No, sir, we have three of us. I forgot to tell you, one of them is down at the entrance hall.\" [235] \"Hmm.\" Inspector Quinn opened his eyes wide and looked kindly at Jesse, \"If the Roman Theater only sells orange juice, how do you explain the presence of this ginger ale bottle here? How do you explain that?\" [236] Officer Quinn suddenly reached down and lifted up the deep green bottle that Hargstrom had found. The boy's face turned pale, and he bit his lips, his eyes darting from side to side, as if searching for an escape route. He then extended a dirty, long finger between his neck and collar and coughed. [237] \"Ah... ah...\" he spoke with difficulty. [238] Officer Quinn put down the bottle, leaning his thin body against the armrest of the chair, his face stern and arms folded. [239] \"What's your name?\" he asked. [240] The boy's face turned from green to white. He glanced discreetly at Hargstrom, who took out a notebook and pencil from his pocket, waiting with a serious expression. [241] The boy licked his lips and answered in a hoarse voice, \"Linchi, Jesse Linchi.\" [242] \"Where were you during intermission, Linchi?\" Detective Quinn asked sternly. [243] \"I...I was just here, in the left alley, sir,\" the boy stammered. [244] \"Ah,\" Detective Quinn said, with his eyebrows tightly furrowed, \"were you selling drinks in the left alley tonight?\" [245] \"What? Ah, yes, sir.\" [246] \"So, you know about the ginger ale bottles, then?\" Detective O'Hara asked again. [247] The boy looked around and spotted Louis Pancher, the short and stout man preparing to speak on stage, and leaned in to whisper, \"Yes, sir, I do. I...I didn't mention it before because Mr. Pancher is very strict about rules, and if he knew, I'd be fired. Sir, you won't tell him, will you?\" [248] Detective O'Hara was startled and chuckled. \"Go on, child. If your conscience is bothering you, tell me.\" [249] \"Sir, here's what happened,\" Jesse Lynch blurted out eagerly. \"Five minutes before the first act ended, I set up my stall here in the left alleyway, just like I always do. After the first act ended, the girl standing in the side hallway opens the door, and we yell out to the crowd coming out. We all do it. A lot of people buy drinks, and I'm so busy that I don't even notice what's going on around me. A few minutes later, just when I'm about to take a breath, a man walks up to me and says, 'Give me a bottle of ginger ale.' I look up and see a man dressed in a fancy evening suit, clearly drunk. He looks happy and laughs to himself. I think to myself, 'I bet I know why he wants ginger ale.' It's obvious because he pats his back pocket and winks at me. Hmm\u2014\"\n[250] \"Hold on a second, child,\" Detective O'Hara interrupted him. \"Have you ever seen a dead body before?\" [251] \"What? Ah, no, sir, I should be fine if I just look at it once,\" the boy said nervously. [252] \"That's good. Is the man who bought the ginger ale the one who died?\" Officer Quinn